WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.653 --> 00:00:09.593
I'm super excited to
introduce Jennifer Rexford.

00:00:09.594 --> 00:00:13.353
Jenn, [LAUGH] as she
allowed me to call her.

00:00:13.354 --> 00:00:19.750
So she's the winner of
the ACM Athena Lecture Award.

00:00:19.751 --> 00:00:21.380
It's Athena Lecture.

00:00:21.381 --> 00:00:25.820
And which is kind of
the Turing Award for women.

00:00:25.821 --> 00:00:28.520
So this is a really,
really big deal.

00:00:28.521 --> 00:00:32.054
And, but of course, this is not
the only award that she has won.

00:00:32.055 --> 00:00:36.514
She won the Grace,
ACM Grace Hopper award in 2005.

00:00:36.515 --> 00:00:38.210
She's an ACM fellow,

00:00:38.211 --> 00:00:42.317
she is a fellow of the American
Association for Arts and

00:00:42.318 --> 00:00:47.060
Sciences and a member of the
National Academy of Engineering.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.971
So she got her PhD from
the University of Michigan,

00:00:51.972 --> 00:00:55.064
as it turns out in the same
year that I got my PhD, so

00:00:55.065 --> 00:00:57.077
I'm not gonna comment on that.

00:00:57.078 --> 00:00:59.112
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:59.113 --> 00:00:59.878
&gt;&gt; For protecting my

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:00.492
own privacy.

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:01.940
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:01:01.941 --> 00:01:07.400
then she went to industry to
work at AT&amp;T and Bell Labs.

00:01:07.401 --> 00:01:12.215
And Albert is here,
and so they go back.

00:01:12.216 --> 00:01:15.643
But of course, she has
a number of other Microsoft

00:01:15.644 --> 00:01:19.399
relationships, so
her students are always welcome,

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.084
and they do come, and
we're very grateful for that.

00:01:23.085 --> 00:01:25.444
This is not her first talk.

00:01:25.445 --> 00:01:30.384
She gave a summer school
on networking in India.

00:01:30.385 --> 00:01:32.087
And as I just heard,

00:01:32.088 --> 00:01:36.606
she visited Victor's group
nine years ago, right?

00:01:36.607 --> 00:01:37.906
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:01:37.907 --> 00:01:43.894
&gt;&gt; Now, after AT&amp;T, she's on
the faculty at Princeton,

00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:48.134
and in her work she's
kind of a pioneer of

00:01:48.135 --> 00:01:51.890
software-defined networks.

00:01:51.891 --> 00:01:55.300
And again, your co-inventor
of OpenFlow, and

00:01:55.301 --> 00:01:58.490
I have a personal history with
that, because when I was at ETH,

00:01:58.491 --> 00:02:04.120
we bought a huge pile
of Arista switches.

00:02:04.121 --> 00:02:07.411
And the big defining factor was
that they were OpenFlow and

00:02:07.412 --> 00:02:09.035
that we could program them.

00:02:09.036 --> 00:02:12.365
Before that,
she also worked on PGP,

00:02:12.366 --> 00:02:15.915
which I also at some
point taught at ETH.

00:02:15.916 --> 00:02:17.555
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:17.556 --> 00:02:18.071
&gt;&gt; And one

00:02:18.072 --> 00:02:19.966
of the most interesting,

00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:24.445
as I was googling and searching
around, she has a page with all

00:02:24.446 --> 00:02:28.250
the advice that she give
like PhD students and so on.

00:02:28.251 --> 00:02:31.627
And there was also one of
our PowerPoint text which is

00:02:31.628 --> 00:02:35.327
interesting for me which was
industry versus academia.

00:02:35.328 --> 00:02:37.990
And this is my last thing.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.820
You went from industry
to academia and

00:02:40.821 --> 00:02:42.580
the PowerPoint explains why.

00:02:42.581 --> 00:02:44.345
I went from academia
to industry.

00:02:44.346 --> 00:02:48.747
So we'll cross our
stories later today.

00:02:48.748 --> 00:02:49.605
&gt;&gt; We'll call it even.

00:02:49.606 --> 00:02:50.827
&gt;&gt; Yeah, okay [LAUGH].

00:02:50.828 --> 00:02:55.230
So thanks for coming and
yeah, and please, we are all-

00:02:55.231 --> 00:02:55.989
&gt;&gt; Thanks so much for

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:59.190
the introduction, and it's great
to be among so many old friends.

00:02:59.191 --> 00:03:02.108
So what I wanna do is give
a talk that I've given at CITCON

00:03:02.109 --> 00:03:04.730
our main networking
conference a few months ago.

00:03:04.731 --> 00:03:08.260
And it's about interdisciplinary
research in computer networking.

00:03:08.261 --> 00:03:10.646
And what I wanna do is give you
a little bit of background of

00:03:10.647 --> 00:03:13.131
how I came into this style of
research, and formed a lot by my

00:03:13.132 --> 00:03:15.621
experiences at AT&amp;T, walk
through a few example projects

00:03:15.622 --> 00:03:17.652
that I've worked on since
going to Princeton.

00:03:17.653 --> 00:03:19.481
And then end with some,
sort of advice for

00:03:19.482 --> 00:03:22.047
people, particularly young
people that are considering

00:03:22.048 --> 00:03:24.508
doing interdisciplinary work,
both about the joys and

00:03:24.509 --> 00:03:26.704
some of the risks of doing
that style of research,

00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:29.320
particularly from
an academic perspective.

00:03:29.321 --> 00:03:31.912
So just to give a kind
of a little context.

00:03:31.913 --> 00:03:34.862
To think all of us are living
in a really exciting time in

00:03:34.863 --> 00:03:36.153
computer networking.

00:03:36.154 --> 00:03:38.715
Really, the Internet is
an invention that escaped from

00:03:38.716 --> 00:03:41.602
the lab during our life times to
become a global communications

00:03:41.603 --> 00:03:43.412
infrastructure, growing
from this.

00:03:43.413 --> 00:03:45.666
It's a really global
communications infrastructure

00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:47.136
that's increasingly critical for

00:03:47.137 --> 00:03:50.000
a huge number of applications
that affect our daily lives.

00:03:50.001 --> 00:03:51.780
And it's not only
a critical infrastructure,

00:03:51.781 --> 00:03:53.930
it's one with near
constant innovation.

00:03:53.931 --> 00:03:56.798
Innovation in the applications
and services that run on top,

00:03:56.799 --> 00:03:59.560
on the kinds of machines that
connect to it at the edge, and

00:03:59.561 --> 00:04:02.090
the kind of physical
infrastructure that the network

00:04:02.091 --> 00:04:03.410
itself runs over.

00:04:03.411 --> 00:04:06.110
So, society critically
depends on it.

00:04:06.111 --> 00:04:07.649
It's in a constant
state of churn.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.406
And so a really simple grand
challenge that we really all,

00:04:10.407 --> 00:04:13.330
I think need to think about, not
just as networking people but

00:04:13.331 --> 00:04:16.084
as computer scientists probably,
is that we need to build

00:04:16.085 --> 00:04:18.897
computer networks that are
worthy of the trust that society

00:04:18.898 --> 00:04:20.381
increasingly places in them.

00:04:20.382 --> 00:04:22.721
And that really just
unpack that a little bit,

00:04:22.722 --> 00:04:24.521
means they needs
to be performant.

00:04:24.522 --> 00:04:27.525
They need to be reliable,
secure, protect user privacy,

00:04:27.526 --> 00:04:28.667
be fair, efficient,

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:31.974
enable the different autonomous
entities on the Internet to work

00:04:31.975 --> 00:04:34.700
somewhat independently
to be cost effective.

00:04:34.701 --> 00:04:37.240
And even reason about
the inherent tensions between

00:04:37.241 --> 00:04:38.390
all of these goals.

00:04:38.391 --> 00:04:39.010
And so, I would argue,

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:41.930
to do that, we really need to
build much stronger intellectual

00:04:41.931 --> 00:04:45.450
foundations for how we design
and operate networks, so

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:47.870
that we can build better
real world networks.

00:04:47.871 --> 00:04:49.855
And also, in particular, build
ones that are robust to the

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:53.500
constant innovation taking place
above, next to, and below them.

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:56.170
And the main point I wanna make
in this talk is that networking

00:04:56.171 --> 00:04:58.830
researchers really can't do this
alone because the intellectual

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:00.350
challenges are so
significant and

00:05:00.351 --> 00:05:02.470
the societal implications so
broad.

00:05:02.471 --> 00:05:03.490
And that's why, really,

00:05:03.491 --> 00:05:05.770
an interdisciplinary approach
to solving these problems is so

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:06.780
critical.

00:05:06.781 --> 00:05:09.624
And so what I wanna talk about
is bringing together these sort

00:05:09.625 --> 00:05:12.413
of big, hairy, audacious
research problems that arise in

00:05:12.414 --> 00:05:15.039
computer networking together
with effective solution

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:17.886
techniques that borrow from
other parts of computer science

00:05:17.887 --> 00:05:20.261
and other areas of
engineering and mathematics.

00:05:20.262 --> 00:05:23.019
And this sort of style of
research was something that was

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.599
born out of my experiences
in the nine years I spent in

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.530
Albert's group at AT&amp;T, where
the group we were in really

00:05:28.531 --> 00:05:31.817
connected the people within the
research organization doing work

00:05:31.818 --> 00:05:34.869
in algorithms, optimization and
statistics with the people

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.312
that were really holding
the network together.

00:05:37.313 --> 00:05:39.970
AT&amp;T's commercial
backbone network,

00:05:39.971 --> 00:05:42.630
together to be able to take the
problems that they were facing

00:05:42.631 --> 00:05:44.810
in their day to day live
running the network and

00:05:44.811 --> 00:05:47.370
to bring those problems
out of the sort of

00:05:47.371 --> 00:05:50.010
great details in the domain
details that networking people

00:05:50.011 --> 00:05:52.560
would be comfortable with in the
problems, there can really be

00:05:52.561 --> 00:05:55.960
a tact in a rigorous way
from other disciplines.

00:05:55.961 --> 00:05:59.840
And so in the work in Albert's
lab, the work started before I

00:05:59.841 --> 00:06:02.950
joined, but I think the premise
of a lot of the work was that

00:06:02.951 --> 00:06:04.830
the network wasn't designed
to be managed, right?

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:06.520
Internet protocols
are really not designed

00:06:06.521 --> 00:06:07.720
with a network operator in mind.

00:06:07.721 --> 00:06:10.590
If anything, network management
and commercial interests

00:06:10.591 --> 00:06:13.140
were anathema to the early
designers of the Internet.

00:06:13.141 --> 00:06:16.240
And so the idea that came out
from Albert and his group really

00:06:16.241 --> 00:06:19.464
worked on was the idea that we
could bolt on network management

00:06:19.465 --> 00:06:21.572
on top by having a sort
of control loop for

00:06:21.573 --> 00:06:24.362
measuring the network to
know that offered traffic,

00:06:24.363 --> 00:06:27.836
the topology, anomalies, taking
place in the network and so on.

00:06:27.837 --> 00:06:30.879
And feed that into systems
that model, analyze,

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:32.116
optimize and so on.

00:06:32.117 --> 00:06:35.470
And use those results to go
back and actually affect change

00:06:35.471 --> 00:06:38.892
in the network, to block
attacks, to re-route traffic,

00:06:38.893 --> 00:06:42.710
to plan for the future by
following this controller.

00:06:42.711 --> 00:06:45.016
So there was a lot of work I was
involved in in the nine years I

00:06:45.017 --> 00:06:45.592
was at AT&amp;T.

00:06:45.593 --> 00:06:48.514
And a lot of it was able to be
both academically interesting,

00:06:48.515 --> 00:06:51.150
and deployed in practice
because it was sort of close to

00:06:51.151 --> 00:06:53.902
those domain details, and yet
abstracted a bit from them

00:06:53.903 --> 00:06:56.572
enough that we could solve
them in more rigorous ways.

00:06:56.573 --> 00:06:59.877
So at the time that I left AT&amp;T,
the one frustration I had with

00:06:59.878 --> 00:07:02.865
this style of work was that for
good reason we were really

00:07:02.866 --> 00:07:06.253
focusing on taking the existing
network equipment as a given.

00:07:06.254 --> 00:07:07.653
This was pragmatic.

00:07:07.654 --> 00:07:08.993
We didn't really have a choice.

00:07:08.994 --> 00:07:11.561
Even AT&amp;T is a major purchase
of network equipment,

00:07:11.562 --> 00:07:14.242
didn't get to control the code
that ran inside the box,

00:07:14.243 --> 00:07:15.793
let alone the hardware inside.

00:07:15.794 --> 00:07:16.736
And so pragmatically,

00:07:16.737 --> 00:07:19.044
it was really difficult to do
much of anything if you wanted

00:07:19.045 --> 00:07:21.273
to do something that required
changing the equipment.

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:24.106
But when I went to Princeton, I
was interested in taking a step

00:07:24.107 --> 00:07:27.160
back and revisiting this control
loop and the problems associated

00:07:27.161 --> 00:07:29.253
with it assuming I could
change the network.

00:07:29.254 --> 00:07:31.304
And there was already at
the time starting to be

00:07:31.305 --> 00:07:33.987
signs of an interest in making
networks more programmable,

00:07:33.988 --> 00:07:36.986
including work that Albert Dave
Moths, myself, and others worked

00:07:36.987 --> 00:07:39.403
on at AT&amp;T that was a part of
this sort of great movement

00:07:39.404 --> 00:07:42.112
towards software defined
networking over the last decade.

00:07:42.113 --> 00:07:44.710
But more generally, I also
just wanted to engage with

00:07:44.711 --> 00:07:47.540
the broader set of colleagues
in different disciplines on

00:07:47.541 --> 00:07:50.369
problems that I hadn't been
able to tackle the way I wanted

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:53.083
because of having an arm held
behind my back by the Legacy

00:07:53.084 --> 00:07:53.742
equipment.

00:07:53.743 --> 00:07:56.831
So what I wanted to talk about
are three example projects that

00:07:56.832 --> 00:07:59.040
I worked on since
coming to Princeton.

00:07:59.041 --> 00:08:01.820
They're kind of are part of a
stack of protocols, if you will,

00:08:01.821 --> 00:08:03.030
for running the network.

00:08:03.031 --> 00:08:05.448
The first looking at
higher-level policies,

00:08:05.449 --> 00:08:08.593
making it easier for network
administrators to describe what

00:08:08.594 --> 00:08:10.225
they want the network to do, and

00:08:10.226 --> 00:08:13.134
to synthesize the configuration
of the network from it.

00:08:13.135 --> 00:08:16.278
One level lower to allow
the distributed collection of

00:08:16.279 --> 00:08:19.489
routers in the network to figure
out what is the right way

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:22.017
to send traffic by
solving together a single

00:08:22.018 --> 00:08:23.946
central optimization problem.

00:08:23.947 --> 00:08:25.775
And then finally
getting the data out of

00:08:25.776 --> 00:08:28.049
the underlying devices
That we need to make sound

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.499
decisions about the network.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:31.290
And then we'll describe these
in chronological order.

00:08:31.291 --> 00:08:33.020
So actually the middle
project took place first.

00:08:33.021 --> 00:08:34.660
I'm going to talk
about that one first.

00:08:34.661 --> 00:08:35.380
But in each of these,

00:08:35.381 --> 00:08:38.030
what I'm going to do is talk
about the problem we looked at.

00:08:38.031 --> 00:08:40.430
Kind of a brief synopsis
of the research results.

00:08:40.431 --> 00:08:43.150
And a little bit of the human
side of the lesson,

00:08:43.151 --> 00:08:44.900
how the collaboration worked.

00:08:44.901 --> 00:08:47.690
So in each of these projects
there was a set of other people

00:08:47.691 --> 00:08:49.930
involved in
programming languages,

00:08:49.931 --> 00:08:53.100
in optimization theory, or in
theoretical computer science in

00:08:53.101 --> 00:08:54.510
the area of compact
data structure.

00:08:54.511 --> 00:08:57.320
And I want to say a little bit
about how those collaborations

00:08:57.321 --> 00:09:00.130
happened, and
what made them possible.

00:09:00.131 --> 00:09:02.620
And at the end I'll talk
a little bit more in anonymized

00:09:02.621 --> 00:09:04.910
terms about less successful
interdisciplinary projects

00:09:04.911 --> 00:09:08.890
I've been involved in, and what
went wrong in those projects.

00:09:08.891 --> 00:09:10.600
But just to start at the middle,

00:09:10.601 --> 00:09:14.490
a lot of the work that we did
at AT&amp;T focused on optimization.

00:09:14.491 --> 00:09:17.240
Right we're trying to optimize
the resources inside the network

00:09:17.241 --> 00:09:18.990
to the prevailing traffic.

00:09:18.991 --> 00:09:20.880
And I became very interested.

00:09:20.881 --> 00:09:22.890
If we were to start with
optimization in mind

00:09:22.891 --> 00:09:23.780
from the beginning.

00:09:23.781 --> 00:09:26.270
If we weren't forced to solve
the optimization problem

00:09:26.271 --> 00:09:28.870
the network itself induced,
could we actually design

00:09:28.871 --> 00:09:31.980
the network to be inherently
easier to optimize?

00:09:31.981 --> 00:09:33.160
And so I engaged with
a colleague of mine

00:09:33.161 --> 00:09:33.780
then at Princeton,

00:09:33.781 --> 00:09:36.390
Mung Chiang, who is an expert
in network optimization.

00:09:36.391 --> 00:09:39.090
And he has been working along
with a number of other people

00:09:39.091 --> 00:09:42.170
on the idea that protocols
are distributed optimizer's.

00:09:42.171 --> 00:09:44.670
That protocols are themselves
a distributed algorithm for

00:09:44.671 --> 00:09:47.500
solving and optimization problem
and that we can turn the crank

00:09:47.501 --> 00:09:50.430
the other way, we can start with
an optimization problem and

00:09:50.431 --> 00:09:51.920
generate the protocol.

00:09:51.921 --> 00:09:53.430
That corresponds to it.

00:09:53.431 --> 00:09:56.360
So the tipping off point was the
projects that I had been working

00:09:56.361 --> 00:09:58.420
on at at&amp;t just before joining.

00:09:58.421 --> 00:09:59.040
Well, we were

00:09:59.041 --> 00:10:01.300
really thinking from the network
operator's perspective,

00:10:01.301 --> 00:10:04.730
how to optimize the flow of
traffic inside the network.

00:10:04.731 --> 00:10:06.306
There we couldn't
change the end hosts,

00:10:06.307 --> 00:10:07.620
we couldn't change the routers.

00:10:07.621 --> 00:10:10.300
The end hosts are sending
traffic in whatever way they're

00:10:10.301 --> 00:10:11.190
gonna send.

00:10:11.191 --> 00:10:13.330
The routers are computing
shortest paths and

00:10:13.331 --> 00:10:16.340
the network operator can only
observed the offered traffic and

00:10:16.341 --> 00:10:17.840
tweak the routing protocols so

00:10:17.841 --> 00:10:21.150
that the routers in doing their
own computational shortest paths

00:10:21.151 --> 00:10:24.600
might compute sensible paths to
the network that balance load.

00:10:24.601 --> 00:10:25.860
But the more general
question is,

00:10:25.861 --> 00:10:28.460
how much traffic should be
sent and over what path?

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:31.760
And what is the right division
of labor between the senders,

00:10:31.761 --> 00:10:33.260
who are adapting
their sending rates,

00:10:33.261 --> 00:10:35.250
the routers that
are computing the paths, and

00:10:35.251 --> 00:10:38.170
the network operator that's
trying to minimize congestion.

00:10:38.171 --> 00:10:40.020
The way that this
was being done.

00:10:40.021 --> 00:10:44.640
At the time was essentially
without any cooperation between

00:10:44.641 --> 00:10:48.420
these three parties, and
hosts, using the TCP or

00:10:48.421 --> 00:10:51.280
Transmission Control Protocol,
would adapt their sending

00:10:51.281 --> 00:10:54.150
rates based on observations
of network congestion.

00:10:54.151 --> 00:10:56.700
When your packets get through
quickly without getting lost,

00:10:56.701 --> 00:10:57.640
send more.

00:10:57.641 --> 00:10:58.420
When they get lost or

00:10:58.421 --> 00:11:02.410
delayed, send less in order to
avoid overloading the network.

00:11:02.411 --> 00:11:04.730
And the network in turn,
with the operator's help,

00:11:04.731 --> 00:11:08.320
would be monitoring the overall
network wide traffic.

00:11:08.321 --> 00:11:11.120
And reoptimizing the routing
to direct traffic over less

00:11:11.121 --> 00:11:12.490
congested paths.

00:11:12.491 --> 00:11:14.500
And so there's a bit of
a control loop happening here if

00:11:14.501 --> 00:11:17.050
these two systems
operate independently.

00:11:17.051 --> 00:11:19.720
Congestion control is changing
the traffic in response to

00:11:19.721 --> 00:11:21.100
observed congestion.

00:11:21.101 --> 00:11:23.650
Traffic engineering is changing
the routes in response to

00:11:23.651 --> 00:11:24.450
observed congestion.

00:11:24.451 --> 00:11:27.570
And they're each optimizing
a slightly different thing, and

00:11:27.571 --> 00:11:29.880
so several things go wrong here.

00:11:29.881 --> 00:11:32.430
There's often a time scale
separation where traffic

00:11:32.431 --> 00:11:35.220
engineering was taking place on
the time scale of hours or day.

00:11:35.221 --> 00:11:38.960
So there was often slow
adaptation to decouple these two

00:11:38.961 --> 00:11:42.600
control loops, and also sort
of a one size fits all view.

00:11:42.601 --> 00:11:45.270
Where the network administrator
would be trying to, in general,

00:11:45.271 --> 00:11:48.370
just minimize congestion, rather
than realizing the different

00:11:48.371 --> 00:11:51.180
applications over the network
might have different notions

00:11:51.181 --> 00:11:53.350
of utility for what they
want the network to do.

00:11:53.351 --> 00:11:57.140
So we took inspiration from
a body of work that came out of

00:11:57.141 --> 00:11:58.800
work from Frank Kelly and
Stephen Lowe and

00:11:58.801 --> 00:12:02.180
others, looking at protocols
as distributed optimizers.

00:12:02.181 --> 00:12:05.090
And so I'm gonna walk you
really briefly through

00:12:05.091 --> 00:12:06.370
the basic idea network.

00:12:06.371 --> 00:12:08.710
It focused a lot on TCP itself.

00:12:08.711 --> 00:12:11.690
So the idea is that the
transmission control protocol

00:12:11.691 --> 00:12:14.360
is solving
an optimization problem.

00:12:14.361 --> 00:12:16.960
Where traffic is being sent
over a route at a particular

00:12:16.961 --> 00:12:18.980
rate from a particular sender.

00:12:18.981 --> 00:12:21.670
And the idea is that sender
wants to send traffic

00:12:21.671 --> 00:12:24.140
at a higher rate to get
higher utility, but

00:12:24.141 --> 00:12:25.750
with diminishing returns.

00:12:25.751 --> 00:12:27.200
So as the sending
rate is higher and

00:12:27.201 --> 00:12:29.570
is delivered over the network,
the user's happier and happier.

00:12:29.571 --> 00:12:31.820
But they're the most happy when
they go from getting nothing to

00:12:31.821 --> 00:12:34.500
getting a little bit more, and
gradually there's diminishing

00:12:34.501 --> 00:12:36.710
returns as they get higher and
higher bandwidth.

00:12:36.711 --> 00:12:40.490
This is sort of a concave
curve of utility.

00:12:40.491 --> 00:12:43.610
And the beautiful work that
Steven and Frank Kelly did and

00:12:43.611 --> 00:12:47.140
others was to recognize
that what TCP is doing,

00:12:47.141 --> 00:12:49.600
even though the early designers
of TCP didn't realized it,

00:12:49.601 --> 00:12:52.140
were solving a utility
maximization problem.

00:12:52.141 --> 00:12:54.940
Where you're essentially trying
to maximize the total utility

00:12:54.941 --> 00:12:57.600
over all senders
subject to these terms.

00:12:57.601 --> 00:13:00.540
And subject to constraints on
the capacities of the like.

00:13:00.541 --> 00:13:03.110
In other words, if we have two
flows, one and two, they're

00:13:03.111 --> 00:13:06.300
gonna have to share that link
l that has some capacity cl.

00:13:06.301 --> 00:13:09.190
And they should do so
in a way where they maximize

00:13:09.191 --> 00:13:12.180
the total utility across the two
users giving each of them

00:13:12.181 --> 00:13:15.050
the bandwidth whoever needs it
most, relative to the utility.

00:13:15.051 --> 00:13:17.160
&gt;&gt; What year was this work?

00:13:17.161 --> 00:13:19.430
&gt;&gt; This would have been back,
let's see,

00:13:19.431 --> 00:13:21.500
around the early 2000s.

00:13:21.501 --> 00:13:24.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah, cuz in 99, 2000, we
&gt;&gt; I chaired an isat study

00:13:24.201 --> 00:13:29.560
looking at future models on the
internet and Scott and the whole

00:13:29.561 --> 00:13:32.210
gang was saying, don't touch and
don't think about TCP IP.

00:13:32.211 --> 00:13:35.570
We don't know how it works,
if you try to do

00:13:35.571 --> 00:13:37.670
coordinated control,
going to mess things up.

00:13:37.671 --> 00:13:39.470
So let's just go with
the lightweight approach, and

00:13:39.471 --> 00:13:43.210
that was kind of the theme back
then, stay away, hands off You

00:13:43.211 --> 00:13:46.620
know, the cross at a distance
don't optimize, just go away.

00:13:46.621 --> 00:13:48.820
And I guess there's
a change in there.

00:13:48.821 --> 00:13:49.321
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:13:49.321 --> 00:13:52.170
I think in the beggining it
changed in a way of being

00:13:52.171 --> 00:13:53.040
reversed engineering.

00:13:53.041 --> 00:13:55.380
Was like, hey we have this sort
of cottage industry of various

00:13:55.381 --> 00:13:58.440
versions of TCP, all named
after geographic locations.

00:13:58.441 --> 00:14:00.940
TCP Madison, etc.

00:14:00.941 --> 00:14:03.579
All of these were eventually
reverse-engineered to fall in

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:04.360
this framework.

00:14:04.361 --> 00:14:06.280
It's actually a really
beautiful body of work, and

00:14:06.281 --> 00:14:09.680
they all differ in exactly what
shape that utility function has.

00:14:09.681 --> 00:14:11.570
And there's a really
beautiful connection to

00:14:11.571 --> 00:14:15.120
the notion of alpha fair utility
functions in network economics.

00:14:15.121 --> 00:14:17.670
And all the different variants
of TCP that are prominent have

00:14:17.671 --> 00:14:20.990
been translated into
what number alpha

00:14:20.991 --> 00:14:22.420
Best describes
the utility function.

00:14:22.421 --> 00:14:24.350
It's actually quite lovely.

00:14:24.351 --> 00:14:25.910
And I don't think
it's a coincidence.

00:14:25.911 --> 00:14:26.840
The hackers, if you will,

00:14:26.841 --> 00:14:30.220
that got TCP working without
the benefit of this theory,

00:14:30.221 --> 00:14:33.300
were doing something based on
intuition that kinda mimics it.

00:14:33.301 --> 00:14:35.860
But it's really lovely that
there's sort of a collapsing of

00:14:35.861 --> 00:14:40.120
the literature on TCP end
of this nice framework.

00:14:40.121 --> 00:14:42.730
So the basic idea at the time
I joined Princeton a number of

00:14:42.731 --> 00:14:45.170
people were starting to turn
the crank the other way right?

00:14:45.171 --> 00:14:47.980
Can we start with this
definition of utility functions

00:14:47.981 --> 00:14:50.200
and generate new
versions of TCP?

00:14:50.201 --> 00:14:52.760
And Steven Lowe
generated one called Fast

00:14:52.761 --> 00:14:56.100
that gave TCP for really long
distance networks where you have

00:14:56.101 --> 00:14:59.790
very slow feedback coming back
from the network and so on.

00:14:59.791 --> 00:15:02.750
So the jumping off point for
me in Mung was to think well

00:15:02.751 --> 00:15:05.220
what if we wanted to control
not only the sending rate but

00:15:05.221 --> 00:15:06.500
also the path?

00:15:06.501 --> 00:15:08.830
So we wanted to solve the entire
traffic engineering problem in

00:15:08.831 --> 00:15:09.930
a unified way.

00:15:09.931 --> 00:15:12.800
So what we started with was
very much the same idea.

00:15:12.801 --> 00:15:15.110
The only thing is I was given
an extra degree of freedom,

00:15:15.111 --> 00:15:17.750
instead of controlling the
sending rate on a given path.

00:15:17.751 --> 00:15:20.710
We have the ability to control
sending rates on multiple paths

00:15:20.711 --> 00:15:22.520
on behalf of a single sender.

00:15:22.521 --> 00:15:25.700
So we still want to maximize
the same notion of utility, but

00:15:25.701 --> 00:15:27.560
the routing itself
is a variable.

00:15:27.561 --> 00:15:30.070
So there might be let's say 2
paths through the network for

00:15:30.071 --> 00:15:31.050
this sender and

00:15:31.051 --> 00:15:34.280
I have the option to set the
rates on both of these paths so

00:15:34.281 --> 00:15:37.450
that the total utility Is
defined by the sum of them.

00:15:37.451 --> 00:15:40.040
And I wanna, again, be able to
arbitrate who gets access to

00:15:40.041 --> 00:15:43.000
which paths, and
at what rate, collectively.

00:15:43.001 --> 00:15:44.260
So, think of this as trying,
now,

00:15:44.261 --> 00:15:47.130
to solve the traffic
engineering, routing, and

00:15:47.131 --> 00:15:49.320
congestion control problem
in a single, unified way.

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:51.970
And so, what did we do?

00:15:51.971 --> 00:15:54.340
Well, we used the same
kind of optimization,

00:15:54.341 --> 00:15:57.370
decomposition techniques that
the previous work on TCP had

00:15:57.371 --> 00:16:01.620
used, to translate that single
central optimization problem

00:16:01.621 --> 00:16:02.930
into a distributed solution.

00:16:02.931 --> 00:16:05.600
And the form that it takes,
is that every link

00:16:05.601 --> 00:16:09.840
computes a price, based on
its own local observed load.

00:16:09.841 --> 00:16:12.090
Less in indication of
the amount of loss for example,

00:16:12.091 --> 00:16:15.320
taking place on this link
because a link is over capacity.

00:16:15.321 --> 00:16:18.590
Imagine sending up that
feedback to the sender and

00:16:18.591 --> 00:16:21.240
the sender will saw the local
optimization problem to update

00:16:21.241 --> 00:16:22.730
the path rates.

00:16:22.731 --> 00:16:25.650
Basic idea, think of it as
I wanna send more because I

00:16:25.651 --> 00:16:28.440
get more utility when I do but
the more I send the higher

00:16:28.441 --> 00:16:31.700
the price I pay because the
price I pay is the load I send.

00:16:31.701 --> 00:16:33.630
Times the price of the pathenon.

00:16:33.631 --> 00:16:36.770
And so if my path is expensive,
I'm gonna send a little bit

00:16:36.771 --> 00:16:39.050
less, because I'm only getting a
little bit of marginal increase

00:16:39.051 --> 00:16:42.230
in utility as I send more and
I'm paying a higher price.

00:16:42.231 --> 00:16:44.629
So every link is locally
computing prices.

00:16:44.630 --> 00:16:47.958
Every sender is locally solving
an optimization problem to find

00:16:47.959 --> 00:16:51.224
the sweet spot, between sending
more to get more utility, and

00:16:51.225 --> 00:16:53.025
sending less to
pay a lower price.

00:16:53.026 --> 00:16:53.977
Yeah.

00:16:53.978 --> 00:16:55.910
&gt;&gt; How sensitive
are these solutions and

00:16:55.911 --> 00:16:58.700
parameters to assumptions
about the utility curves?

00:16:58.701 --> 00:17:01.850
Do you measure utility directly,
do you measure the curves

00:17:01.851 --> 00:17:05.474
directly and use them or
do you just assume the curves?

00:17:05.475 --> 00:17:07.270
&gt;&gt; We're designing
with a particular

00:17:07.271 --> 00:17:08.340
utility function in mind.

00:17:08.341 --> 00:17:09.690
&gt;&gt; So
how sensitive of what you do

00:17:09.691 --> 00:17:11.800
to the functions of that curve?

00:17:11.801 --> 00:17:13.100
&gt;&gt; It is relatively sensitive.

00:17:13.101 --> 00:17:15.110
You wanna pick something
that has the shape you want,

00:17:15.111 --> 00:17:18.650
because as long as the
optimization problem's convex-

00:17:18.651 --> 00:17:19.570
&gt;&gt; No, I mentioned convex as

00:17:19.571 --> 00:17:20.460
the beginning-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:20.461 --> 00:17:24.470
&gt;&gt; But when it comes to
the parameters of the curve it's

00:17:24.471 --> 00:17:26.200
just very sensitive to that,
and you have to check that,

00:17:26.201 --> 00:17:28.799
and actually we just
go with the assumption

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:31.340
&gt;&gt; So there is a sensitivity.

00:17:31.341 --> 00:17:33.310
I'm not sure I'm getting quite
at your question but there-

00:17:33.311 --> 00:17:34.921
&gt;&gt; Sensitivity to the

00:17:34.922 --> 00:17:35.670
&gt;&gt; There is a sensitivity

00:17:35.671 --> 00:17:37.650
in convergence time.

00:17:37.651 --> 00:17:40.770
That was one and I'll come
to that in a few slide.

00:17:40.771 --> 00:17:43.790
The sort of brute force kind
of approach of just doing

00:17:43.791 --> 00:17:47.950
decomposition and running ends
up being quite sensitive to,

00:17:47.951 --> 00:17:49.420
it often converts very slowly.

00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:52.760
Even though it is sort of
preferably optimal and

00:17:52.761 --> 00:17:54.650
will converge,
it might converge very slowly.

00:17:54.651 --> 00:17:57.610
And so we found this sort of
straightforward application

00:17:57.611 --> 00:18:00.750
of this technique didn't produce
effective results by itself.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:07.010
So the basic idea, then, is now
we've got a single optimization

00:18:07.011 --> 00:18:09.180
problem that's being solved
by each of the links and

00:18:09.181 --> 00:18:12.120
each of the senders doing
its own local computation.

00:18:12.121 --> 00:18:14.150
So the next question we
cared about was, well,

00:18:14.151 --> 00:18:16.510
what if we have multiple
classes of traffic,

00:18:16.511 --> 00:18:18.760
each with different
performance goals.

00:18:18.761 --> 00:18:21.710
So you might imagine some
traffic cares about throughput.

00:18:21.711 --> 00:18:24.370
Some cares about latency,
and you might have different

00:18:24.371 --> 00:18:26.670
functions for
those different traffic classes.

00:18:26.671 --> 00:18:29.332
And you might imagine that the
links have a share of them with

00:18:29.333 --> 00:18:32.060
allocated the one and
a with allocated to the other.

00:18:32.061 --> 00:18:35.280
Think of this as a green
virtual network for

00:18:35.281 --> 00:18:37.560
the latency sensitive traffic,
and a red one for

00:18:37.561 --> 00:18:39.090
the throughput
sensitive traffic.

00:18:39.091 --> 00:18:40.870
And you might imagine
a straightforward

00:18:40.871 --> 00:18:43.370
extension to the utility
function as you wanna maximize

00:18:43.371 --> 00:18:46.590
the aggregate utility across
the two classes of traffic.

00:18:46.591 --> 00:18:49.100
So again here,
we can turn that crank

00:18:49.101 --> 00:18:52.920
of decomposing this optimization
problem now in two steps.

00:18:52.921 --> 00:18:55.840
One that separates the two
utilities from one another.

00:18:55.841 --> 00:18:58.790
Subject to the local
link capacity shares.

00:18:58.791 --> 00:19:01.520
And a second on a longer
time scale that decides what

00:19:01.521 --> 00:19:05.310
fraction of each link's capacity
should be devoted to each one.

00:19:05.311 --> 00:19:07.400
Think of this as sort of
a form of adaptive network

00:19:07.401 --> 00:19:08.110
virtualization.

00:19:08.111 --> 00:19:10.980
The topology is virtualized but
the bandwidth allocation

00:19:10.981 --> 00:19:13.940
between the two classes are
being dynamically adapted based

00:19:13.941 --> 00:19:17.420
on who needs it more to be able
to achieve their utility goals.

00:19:17.421 --> 00:19:20.790
So, again, same basic idea, that
now we can essentially design

00:19:20.791 --> 00:19:22.580
the same protocol
we would have run,

00:19:22.581 --> 00:19:25.300
had this delay sensitive, or
throughput sensitive traffic

00:19:25.301 --> 00:19:27.640
been the only only traffic
class in the network.

00:19:27.641 --> 00:19:30.230
But we can arbitrate between
them by dynamically deciding

00:19:30.231 --> 00:19:32.110
what shares of them
would they each get.

00:19:32.111 --> 00:19:34.140
So, that was sort
of the second step.

00:19:34.141 --> 00:19:36.950
Now, I think for me,
the lessons here.

00:19:36.951 --> 00:19:39.540
One was the idea that
protocol should be

00:19:39.541 --> 00:19:41.230
solving a well-stated problem.

00:19:41.231 --> 00:19:42.990
This sort of sounds obvious
in hindsight right, but

00:19:42.991 --> 00:19:45.590
a lot of networking protocols
were designed without a really

00:19:45.591 --> 00:19:47.570
clear specification of what
problem they were solving.

00:19:47.571 --> 00:19:50.450
And in particular, what global
problem they were solving rather

00:19:50.451 --> 00:19:51.780
than the local problem.

00:19:51.781 --> 00:19:54.050
A particular sender
might be interested in.

00:19:54.051 --> 00:19:57.110
And then to use techniques for
decomposing the problem.

00:19:57.111 --> 00:19:57.910
This gives us, I think,

00:19:57.911 --> 00:20:00.210
both a better understand of
how the protocols work and

00:20:00.211 --> 00:20:03.320
when they work, and also
guarantees that come from that

00:20:03.321 --> 00:20:06.260
on optimality, and
conversions, and so on.

00:20:06.261 --> 00:20:09.210
But in practice as Erik's
question already hinted at that

00:20:09.211 --> 00:20:12.360
isn't quite enough, the process
itself is inherently iterative.

00:20:12.361 --> 00:20:15.230
We didn't really come to the
solutions we did in one step and

00:20:15.231 --> 00:20:16.820
so there's sort of
a human lesson here and

00:20:16.821 --> 00:20:18.520
then a few technical takeaways.

00:20:18.521 --> 00:20:21.440
One I think of as sort of
research as decomposition

00:20:21.441 --> 00:20:23.250
which is this is sort of
the way the work between Mung

00:20:23.251 --> 00:20:23.910
and I would go.

00:20:23.911 --> 00:20:26.770
I would say I need the network
to solve this problem,

00:20:26.771 --> 00:20:29.060
he would say the math
is too hard.

00:20:29.061 --> 00:20:30.570
And this is great for

00:20:30.571 --> 00:20:32.090
Mung, because instead
of Mung going and

00:20:32.091 --> 00:20:34.310
working on a hard math problem
he would turn back to me and

00:20:34.311 --> 00:20:37.740
say change the problem so
the math is easy.

00:20:37.741 --> 00:20:40.150
He liked to put it as, if you're
the professor and the student,

00:20:40.151 --> 00:20:42.620
you write an easy exam, right?

00:20:42.621 --> 00:20:45.030
So it was my job to figure
out if I could change

00:20:45.031 --> 00:20:48.050
the constraints that I felt the
network would have to impose and

00:20:48.051 --> 00:20:50.610
relax them in some way to
make the problem easier.

00:20:50.611 --> 00:20:53.130
And then Mung would eventually
say thumbs up, it's convex,

00:20:53.131 --> 00:20:57.530
I'm happy, we can apply standard
optimization techniques

00:20:57.531 --> 00:20:58.910
to solve the problem now.

00:20:58.911 --> 00:21:01.160
So the art in this
work was not the math.

00:21:01.161 --> 00:21:04.330
Quite the opposite, the art was
in not making the math hard.

00:21:04.331 --> 00:21:08.510
So just to give two examples of
where that concretely came up.

00:21:08.511 --> 00:21:11.060
One was that we found when
we did the sort of standard

00:21:11.061 --> 00:21:13.820
decomposition approach we did
get something that was optimal

00:21:13.821 --> 00:21:17.080
and conversed, but it conversed
really, really slowly.

00:21:17.081 --> 00:21:19.960
And so we actually changed the
utility function we were using

00:21:19.961 --> 00:21:22.930
accordingly to make it a little
bit quicker in adapting to

00:21:22.931 --> 00:21:24.080
congestion.

00:21:24.081 --> 00:21:26.590
In particular, instead of
looking just at utility,

00:21:26.591 --> 00:21:29.260
we also look at a penalty
function that penalize links

00:21:29.261 --> 00:21:31.600
that were getting close
to their capacity.

00:21:31.601 --> 00:21:34.990
This problem on the left,
maximizing utility was what

00:21:34.991 --> 00:21:38.130
used to be congestion
control was doing.

00:21:38.131 --> 00:21:39.840
Minimizing this penalty was

00:21:39.841 --> 00:21:42.160
actually what the network
operators were doing.

00:21:42.161 --> 00:21:43.300
In doing traffic engineering, so

00:21:43.301 --> 00:21:45.940
there's sort of an interesting
coming full circle here,

00:21:45.941 --> 00:21:48.960
that in some ways,
both sets of hackers were right.

00:21:48.961 --> 00:21:50.670
Maximizing utility and

00:21:50.671 --> 00:21:54.390
keeping a damper on congestion
both end up being important.

00:21:54.391 --> 00:21:56.460
And once we decomposed
this problem,

00:21:56.461 --> 00:21:59.420
we ended up with something that
not only provably optimal and

00:21:59.421 --> 00:22:01.580
stable, but converged much,
much more quickly,

00:22:01.581 --> 00:22:03.410
because it gave an early
warning when the network

00:22:03.411 --> 00:22:05.350
was getting into
an unhappy place.

00:22:05.351 --> 00:22:08.330
Rather than waiting for all
hell to break loose beforehand.

00:22:08.331 --> 00:22:12.000
And a second example, when we're
looking at multiple classes of

00:22:12.001 --> 00:22:14.840
traffic, I described it
as if there's sorta two

00:22:14.841 --> 00:22:17.940
separate virtual networks each
with its own share of resources.

00:22:17.941 --> 00:22:21.800
But when we started we
envisioned, there being one Q,

00:22:21.801 --> 00:22:24.670
one capacity for the link and
the traffic mixing together.

00:22:24.671 --> 00:22:27.210
But in practice that made
the optimization problem

00:22:27.211 --> 00:22:28.820
impossible to decompose.

00:22:28.821 --> 00:22:30.985
Because the utility of one
traffic class was really

00:22:30.986 --> 00:22:33.050
intertwined with
utility of the other.

00:22:33.051 --> 00:22:34.900
To put that in kinda
concrete terms,

00:22:34.901 --> 00:22:38.940
delay sensitive traffic will
be suffering delays in queuing

00:22:38.941 --> 00:22:41.540
because the throughput sensitive
traffic would drive the links to

00:22:41.541 --> 00:22:43.620
very high levels of utilization.

00:22:43.621 --> 00:22:45.640
And so, we found to get
the optimization problem to

00:22:45.641 --> 00:22:47.949
decompose, we had to
have separate queues

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:52.950
on a longer time scale with
the scheduling of them changing

00:22:52.951 --> 00:22:55.460
to accommodate who
needed more utility.

00:22:55.461 --> 00:22:57.540
And that came out of just
observing that the math

00:22:57.541 --> 00:23:00.910
was really hard if we didn't
make that separation.

00:23:00.911 --> 00:23:03.800
So the perfect example where
Mung said can't do it but if we,

00:23:03.801 --> 00:23:05.750
actually could separate this
through traffic classes and

00:23:05.751 --> 00:23:08.720
make their utility functions
less dependent on some time

00:23:08.721 --> 00:23:09.770
scale we can.

00:23:11.130 --> 00:23:13.160
A sort of more human lesson

00:23:13.161 --> 00:23:15.720
here is also anybody who's
ever been a graduate student

00:23:15.721 --> 00:23:17.680
in this room knows that
research never works this way,

00:23:17.681 --> 00:23:20.990
with faculty actually talking to
each other on a regular basis.

00:23:20.991 --> 00:23:23.850
So you should already be quite
suspicious that I haven't told

00:23:23.851 --> 00:23:24.970
you the whole story.

00:23:24.971 --> 00:23:28.380
Which is of course, the student
in the room was critical,

00:23:28.381 --> 00:23:31.232
because she was able to
connect with both of us and

00:23:31.233 --> 00:23:32.624
to really wrangle us and

00:23:32.625 --> 00:23:36.383
get enough expertise with both
topics, of network hardware and

00:23:36.384 --> 00:23:39.935
software constrains as well as
the optimization techniques

00:23:39.936 --> 00:23:43.163
involved to be able to actually
make this work happen.

00:23:43.164 --> 00:23:44.611
Now, for me,
a great frustration,

00:23:44.612 --> 00:23:46.248
this work happened
about 10 years ago,

00:23:46.249 --> 00:23:48.585
is it was really hard to get it
deployed because again we were

00:23:48.586 --> 00:23:51.018
still stuck with the problem I
mentioned at the beginning that

00:23:51.019 --> 00:23:53.120
we can't change the equipment
inside the network.

00:23:53.121 --> 00:23:55.290
So, we could to experiments,
we could do simulations,

00:23:55.291 --> 00:23:56.750
but we couldn't deploy.

00:23:56.751 --> 00:23:59.440
And now we're actually just
starting to have the kind of

00:23:59.441 --> 00:24:01.190
programmable switch hardware.

00:24:01.191 --> 00:24:04.254
And I'll talk more about these
kind of switching capabilities

00:24:04.255 --> 00:24:07.199
a little later in the talk where
now it actually is possible to

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:08.546
deploy these techniques.

00:24:08.547 --> 00:24:11.424
And I'm starting to work with
folks at AT&amp;T, sort of then

00:24:11.425 --> 00:24:14.362
coming full circle who are
interested in doing a deployment

00:24:14.363 --> 00:24:17.122
of some of these ideas to see
if they might be suitable for

00:24:17.123 --> 00:24:18.440
their backbone network.

00:24:20.460 --> 00:24:22.200
So that's sort of the end of
the first part of the talk.

00:24:22.201 --> 00:24:24.400
If we wanna maybe stop here,
if people have comments or

00:24:24.401 --> 00:24:27.510
questions before I go on?

00:24:27.511 --> 00:24:28.390
Yeah?

00:24:28.391 --> 00:24:32.660
&gt;&gt; So you mentioned optimization
as being as the talent

00:24:32.661 --> 00:24:34.470
you're looking for
to mix with networking.

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:39.870
But it seems it's a big third
bubble coming in in mechanism

00:24:39.871 --> 00:24:43.820
design in economics, which is
kind of a different way of

00:24:43.821 --> 00:24:48.580
thinking about distribution of
effort, and credit assignment,

00:24:48.581 --> 00:24:49.490
and payment, and so on.

00:24:49.491 --> 00:24:52.968
I'm curious if that's still
an area that's waiting,

00:24:52.969 --> 00:24:55.641
pregnant with results
in this space, or?

00:24:55.642 --> 00:24:56.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:24:56.951 --> 00:24:59.656
So, yes, I think so, and one
piece of work I don't have in

00:24:59.657 --> 00:25:02.312
here that had that flavor was
looking at how the different

00:25:02.313 --> 00:25:04.691
domains that make up
the Internet interact with one

00:25:04.692 --> 00:25:07.820
another, because their economics
obviously play a direct role.

00:25:07.821 --> 00:25:09.911
Entire autonomous systems,
entire networks,

00:25:09.912 --> 00:25:11.186
are sort of rational actors.

00:25:11.187 --> 00:25:11.755
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:11.756 --> 00:25:14.368
&gt;&gt; Making best responses based
on what their neighbors do.

00:25:14.369 --> 00:25:17.603
And there the language of game
theory is a beautiful fit

00:25:17.604 --> 00:25:18.245
and it is.

00:25:18.246 --> 00:25:22.802
&gt;&gt; Is it an area that is wide
open in the fifteen years

00:25:22.803 --> 00:25:26.579
while I was asleep at
the switch per say,

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:31.819
will that also happen in
the field I've been tracking.

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:34.546
&gt;&gt; I think some, particularly in
the area of inter-domain routing

00:25:34.547 --> 00:25:36.876
I think so, also in areas
around spectrum management or

00:25:36.877 --> 00:25:38.561
in dealing with
scarcity of IP address,

00:25:38.562 --> 00:25:40.792
there's sort of been little
pockets of networking,

00:25:40.793 --> 00:25:42.750
where we've seen game
theory come into play.

00:25:42.751 --> 00:25:45.270
I think there's a great
opportunity to do more there,

00:25:45.271 --> 00:25:47.920
frankly because I think a lot of
the research on game theory has,

00:25:47.921 --> 00:25:49.280
understandably, focused on,

00:25:49.281 --> 00:25:51.190
particularly computer
science work on game theory,

00:25:51.191 --> 00:25:52.600
on some of
the theoretical machinery.

00:25:52.601 --> 00:25:55.610
And there hasn't been enough
of a meeting of the minds with

00:25:55.611 --> 00:25:56.870
practical problems.

00:25:56.871 --> 00:25:59.867
&gt;&gt; But even in the problems
you're putting up here, first

00:25:59.868 --> 00:26:03.325
reaction is, yes we have to do
optimizations of various kinds.

00:26:03.326 --> 00:26:05.300
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:26:05.301 --> 00:26:07.610
&gt;&gt; Classical models,
looking at the whole system, but

00:26:07.611 --> 00:26:09.560
then there's this kind of this
whole distributed world of

00:26:09.561 --> 00:26:12.530
national actors that should be
like, it's like a generation

00:26:12.531 --> 00:26:14.411
approach to thinking
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:26:14.412 --> 00:26:16.839
&gt;&gt; The large scale of
populations of actors,

00:26:16.840 --> 00:26:18.255
as nodes and messages for

00:26:18.256 --> 00:26:21.300
example and in that case
that's wide open right now.

00:26:21.301 --> 00:26:22.250
&gt;&gt; I think it's mid way open,

00:26:22.251 --> 00:26:24.860
I think the reason it doesn't
come up here as much is because

00:26:24.861 --> 00:26:27.300
we are focusing on single
administrative domains.

00:26:27.301 --> 00:26:30.452
Where there are social welfare
maximization is a reasonable

00:26:30.453 --> 00:26:32.677
model, but
certainly spectrum options,

00:26:32.678 --> 00:26:36.091
any sort of distributed scarce
resource I think it's very cool.

00:26:36.092 --> 00:26:38.800
And I think another thing that's
interesting here is a lot of

00:26:38.801 --> 00:26:41.509
the work on game theory doesn't
think it's virtually about

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:44.280
dynamics as say, work in
distributed computing does.

00:26:44.281 --> 00:26:45.820
And yet, a lot of
the distributed computing work

00:26:45.821 --> 00:26:48.030
doesn't have the same sort
of take on rational actors.

00:26:48.031 --> 00:26:50.680
So there's, I think, a ships
in the night problem there,

00:26:50.681 --> 00:26:54.430
where those two fields could be
closer together than they are.

00:26:54.431 --> 00:26:55.180
Yeah, great question.

00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:57.600
Other thoughts?

00:26:58.840 --> 00:27:00.560
So I'll talk about
the second piece of work.

00:27:00.561 --> 00:27:02.890
So one thing that was
comfortable for me in this work

00:27:02.891 --> 00:27:05.170
with while I'm no expert
in network optimization,

00:27:05.171 --> 00:27:07.970
I'm at least familiar with sort
of the language of optimization.

00:27:07.971 --> 00:27:10.150
The next project was a little
bit out of my comfort zone,

00:27:10.151 --> 00:27:12.530
which is working with folks
in programming languages.

00:27:12.531 --> 00:27:15.680
And the jumping off point for
this work was that there was

00:27:15.681 --> 00:27:18.970
a lot of interest around ten
years ago, in moving towards

00:27:18.971 --> 00:27:22.080
a more central model of network
control and management, software

00:27:22.081 --> 00:27:24.130
defined networking, as was
mentioned in the introduction.

00:27:24.131 --> 00:27:26.915
It was something we started
working on a bit when I was at

00:27:26.916 --> 00:27:30.410
AT&amp;T and became more prominent
when open flow came around.

00:27:30.411 --> 00:27:32.750
And so we became interested
in the question of how should

00:27:32.751 --> 00:27:34.936
network administrators
subscribe to the network,

00:27:34.937 --> 00:27:37.780
in a central way.

00:27:37.781 --> 00:27:40.550
How that should describe how the
network should actually behave?

00:27:40.551 --> 00:27:41.530
OpenFlow itself,

00:27:41.531 --> 00:27:44.060
as I'll talk about in a moment
is not a linguistic formalism,

00:27:44.061 --> 00:27:45.070
it's like assembly language.

00:27:45.071 --> 00:27:48.470
It's better than microcode, but
it's still pretty low level.

00:27:48.471 --> 00:27:50.410
And so, I talk a little
bit about this project,

00:27:50.411 --> 00:27:52.360
the Frenetic project,
that was done with Dave Walker,

00:27:52.361 --> 00:27:54.500
my colleague at Princeton,
in Programming Languages, and

00:27:54.501 --> 00:27:56.110
Nate Foster,
who is a Professor at Cornell,

00:27:56.111 --> 00:27:58.998
who was a post-doc at
the time in our groups.

00:27:58.999 --> 00:28:00.720
So, in Software-Defined
Networking, now,

00:28:00.721 --> 00:28:03.900
you sort of pop up a little from
what I described before, and

00:28:03.901 --> 00:28:07.340
now the underlying network
devices, are largely done.

00:28:07.341 --> 00:28:10.180
You have a logically centralized
controller that is running

00:28:10.181 --> 00:28:11.660
some sort of application,

00:28:11.661 --> 00:28:13.740
that is gonna tell the
underlying switches what to do.

00:28:13.741 --> 00:28:17.280
And so that two basic ideas here
one is the idea that decisions

00:28:17.281 --> 00:28:20.500
should be made based on
network wide visibility.

00:28:20.501 --> 00:28:23.310
And exercising network
wide control and

00:28:23.311 --> 00:28:25.870
that you should do so using
some direct open interface to

00:28:25.871 --> 00:28:28.270
the underlined forward
capabilities of the switch.

00:28:28.271 --> 00:28:29.830
This is what was lacking
when I was at AT&amp;T,

00:28:29.831 --> 00:28:32.850
when we were not allowed to
change the equipment,not even

00:28:32.851 --> 00:28:35.240
the software of the equipment,
this changed when there started

00:28:35.241 --> 00:28:37.730
the open interface between
the software on the device and

00:28:37.731 --> 00:28:40.830
the low level hardware that
forwards packets at high speeds.

00:28:40.831 --> 00:28:42.770
And so open flow which
came out around 2008,

00:28:42.771 --> 00:28:46.330
was essentially a beginning
of thinking about what these

00:28:46.331 --> 00:28:48.540
interfaces to the switch
hardware might be.

00:28:48.541 --> 00:28:49.230
So at a high level,

00:28:49.231 --> 00:28:52.600
what OpenFlow 1.0, the original
version of OpenFlow did,

00:28:52.601 --> 00:28:55.970
was to say well every
switch has a rule table.

00:28:55.971 --> 00:28:58.450
Packets come in, they get
matched against the highest

00:28:58.451 --> 00:29:01.160
priority rule based on
patterns in the packet header.

00:29:01.161 --> 00:29:03.250
And the patterns
expressing rules and

00:29:03.251 --> 00:29:05.370
the simple action associated
with the matching rules

00:29:05.371 --> 00:29:06.730
is the one that gets performed.

00:29:06.731 --> 00:29:08.560
So a good example is
a packet comes in,

00:29:08.561 --> 00:29:10.200
you might look at this
prioritized list and

00:29:10.201 --> 00:29:13.050
based on the source IP address
if it starts with the first 2

00:29:13.051 --> 00:29:16.617
octets of 1 &amp; 2 and destination
starts with the first 3 octets

00:29:16.618 --> 00:29:19.300
of 3, 4, and 5, then those
packets would be dropped.

00:29:19.301 --> 00:29:20.710
If the packet doesn't
match this pattern,

00:29:20.711 --> 00:29:22.620
it will fall through to
the second rule, and

00:29:22.621 --> 00:29:25.580
maybe be forwarded out of a port
two or maybe it follows to

00:29:25.581 --> 00:29:28.970
the third rule and get sent to
some sort of off port control.

00:29:28.971 --> 00:29:32.940
So basic idea here, this
actually very cute idea, is that

00:29:32.941 --> 00:29:35.880
all of these marketing terms
that networking is frankly rife

00:29:35.881 --> 00:29:40.520
with, router, switch, firewall,
load balancing, net, firewall.

00:29:40.521 --> 00:29:42.950
All of these things fall
into this basic framework.

00:29:42.951 --> 00:29:44.990
If you match on the MAC address,
you're a switch.

00:29:44.991 --> 00:29:46.800
If you match on the IP address,
you're a router.

00:29:46.801 --> 00:29:49.380
If you're doing access control
based on several of these

00:29:49.381 --> 00:29:50.910
fields, you're a firewall.

00:29:50.911 --> 00:29:52.420
If you're modifying
some of the fields,

00:29:52.421 --> 00:29:54.560
you might be a network
address translator.

00:29:54.561 --> 00:29:57.670
But that the underlying hardware
mechanism is largely the same,

00:29:57.671 --> 00:30:00.310
a rule table with
patterns of matching and

00:30:00.311 --> 00:30:01.580
very simple actions.

00:30:01.581 --> 00:30:05.100
And by taking that interface
that already matched what

00:30:05.101 --> 00:30:06.650
the underlying hardware
was actually doing.

00:30:06.651 --> 00:30:08.600
So this is not designing
new hardware it's just

00:30:08.601 --> 00:30:11.440
opening up the API to
the underlying hardware

00:30:12.590 --> 00:30:13.770
that existed at the time.

00:30:13.771 --> 00:30:15.600
And in particular, a particular
kind of memory called

00:30:15.601 --> 00:30:17.510
the ternary
contact-addressable memory

00:30:17.511 --> 00:30:19.790
that allows these lookups to
take place at very high speed by

00:30:19.791 --> 00:30:22.670
doing them in parallel across
all the rules in the switch.

00:30:22.671 --> 00:30:25.660
So there is sort of a jumping
point that you have now this

00:30:25.661 --> 00:30:28.930
open interface to this wedge,
but it still extremely low level

00:30:28.931 --> 00:30:31.460
as I mentioned a moment
ago OpenFlow mechanism,

00:30:31.461 --> 00:30:32.830
not a linguistic formalism.

00:30:32.831 --> 00:30:35.730
It' s a away to install rules
and switches and if the reason

00:30:35.731 --> 00:30:38.410
about those rules would do to
your packets, but you're still

00:30:38.411 --> 00:30:41.360
thinking about priorities of
rules and wild cards and.

00:30:41.361 --> 00:30:45.470
Thick masks and overlapping
rules and so on, making it

00:30:45.471 --> 00:30:48.460
very difficult to actually
write applications on top.

00:30:48.461 --> 00:30:51.170
And so what we really wanted to
do, is come up with an effective

00:30:51.171 --> 00:30:53.960
programming abstraction that
will let people write their apps

00:30:53.961 --> 00:30:57.490
without having to be experts in
the lingua franca of OpenFlow.

00:30:57.491 --> 00:30:58.280
And in particular,

00:30:58.281 --> 00:31:01.270
we should've started with
the question of marginality.

00:31:01.271 --> 00:31:02.310
Some of the job problems.

00:31:02.311 --> 00:31:05.700
So, you wanna be able to write
an application for your network.

00:31:05.701 --> 00:31:07.520
But actually,
you really wanna write multiple.

00:31:07.521 --> 00:31:09.990
You wanna route, and monitor,
and do firewall, and

00:31:09.991 --> 00:31:11.910
load balancing, and
various other things.

00:31:11.911 --> 00:31:13.120
But most importantly,

00:31:13.121 --> 00:31:15.240
you wanna do all of them
on the same traffic.

00:31:15.241 --> 00:31:17.530
It's not you wanna route some
traffic and monitor other.

00:31:17.531 --> 00:31:20.480
You actually want the illusion
that you're doing this on all of

00:31:20.481 --> 00:31:23.340
the traffic and yet
still be able to avoid building

00:31:23.341 --> 00:31:26.000
a monolithic application that
would be naturally hard to test,

00:31:26.001 --> 00:31:27.200
debug, and so on.

00:31:27.201 --> 00:31:30.038
That sort of motherhood in apple
pie, right, you want modular,

00:31:30.039 --> 00:31:32.157
the ability to develop
modular application, so

00:31:32.158 --> 00:31:34.132
you can write each of
these apps separately.

00:31:34.133 --> 00:31:36.971
But because each of them
partially specify how the same

00:31:36.972 --> 00:31:39.993
traffic should be handled,
you can't just run all four of

00:31:39.994 --> 00:31:42.225
them with isolation
mechanisms in place.

00:31:42.226 --> 00:31:44.915
You need to be able to reason
about their composition.

00:31:44.916 --> 00:31:45.585
So you can write and

00:31:45.586 --> 00:31:48.295
reason about them separately and
yet still have them act as

00:31:48.296 --> 00:31:50.915
a single application on
the single set of traffic

00:31:50.916 --> 00:31:54.430
affecting a single table of
rules in the underlying switch.

00:31:54.431 --> 00:31:56.906
So that's the problem we
were trying to solve.

00:31:56.907 --> 00:31:58.860
So my colleagues and
programming languages,

00:31:58.861 --> 00:32:01.130
like to think about
everything as a function and

00:32:01.131 --> 00:32:02.960
everything is what,
composition of functions,

00:32:02.961 --> 00:32:05.515
and so a functional reactive
programming was the area of

00:32:05.516 --> 00:32:08.040
that, we started looking
at to give us ideas.

00:32:08.041 --> 00:32:11.150
So the first idea is how can
you model OpenFlow itself?

00:32:11.151 --> 00:32:13.350
So you can think of
OpenFlow as a function.

00:32:13.351 --> 00:32:16.040
A function from a packet
to a set of packets.

00:32:16.041 --> 00:32:18.910
So packet and its location,
this is all the header fields

00:32:18.911 --> 00:32:22.120
including something logical
about where the packet is at

00:32:22.121 --> 00:32:24.870
this moment, what switch and
what part of the switch are at.

00:32:24.871 --> 00:32:27.500
And they're gonna do
a function on this packet

00:32:27.501 --> 00:32:30.570
that would generate some set of
packets, the reason that it's

00:32:30.571 --> 00:32:33.390
a set is if you drop the packet,
it might become an empty set.

00:32:33.391 --> 00:32:34.360
If you forward the packet,

00:32:34.361 --> 00:32:36.890
it might become a new
packet at a new location.

00:32:36.891 --> 00:32:38.230
If you multi cast the packet,

00:32:38.231 --> 00:32:41.190
it might be multiple packets
at multiple locations.

00:32:41.191 --> 00:32:43.790
So the basic idea is you have
a packet and it's location,

00:32:43.791 --> 00:32:45.990
and then you're going to spit
out a set of packets and

00:32:45.991 --> 00:32:46.738
their locations.

00:32:46.739 --> 00:32:49.330
And that's gonna mimic
what operation the switch

00:32:49.331 --> 00:32:50.860
is actually performing
on the packets.

00:32:50.861 --> 00:32:53.418
Because that's sort of
the first basic idea.

00:32:53.419 --> 00:32:55.379
And just to give us
really simple example,

00:32:55.380 --> 00:32:58.428
you might imagine having Boolean
predicates that match on certain

00:32:58.429 --> 00:33:01.220
aspects of the packet's
location and heterofields.

00:33:01.221 --> 00:33:03.530
And then do modifications to
the packet's location and

00:33:03.531 --> 00:33:06.380
heterofields accordingly, to do
packet forwarding, or in this

00:33:06.381 --> 00:33:09.840
case also netting, by changing
the packet's destination IP.

00:33:09.841 --> 00:33:11.460
Yeah, that's sort
of the first idea.

00:33:11.461 --> 00:33:14.300
The second is now you've go this
functional way of thinking about

00:33:14.301 --> 00:33:17.838
policy, you can do sort of
standard kinds of composition.

00:33:17.839 --> 00:33:20.030
And in particularly, you could
imagine that two basic ideas of

00:33:20.031 --> 00:33:22.490
doing composition in
parallel and in series.

00:33:22.491 --> 00:33:24.160
So just to give
a concrete example,

00:33:24.161 --> 00:33:27.230
let's suppose I'm gonna monitor
traffic by source IP address.

00:33:27.231 --> 00:33:30.039
I wanna give you the histogram
of the traffic by a sender.

00:33:30.040 --> 00:33:32.902
And I wanna route the traffic
based on the address block it's

00:33:32.903 --> 00:33:33.580
destined to.

00:33:33.581 --> 00:33:35.624
So I wanna route on
destination prefix.

00:33:35.625 --> 00:33:38.862
Then I might have one function
that tells me to count traffic

00:33:38.863 --> 00:33:41.080
depending on the value
of the source IP.

00:33:41.081 --> 00:33:43.634
And I might have a second
function that wants to forward

00:33:43.635 --> 00:33:46.354
traffic out of a particular
output ort based on the value of

00:33:46.355 --> 00:33:47.395
the destination IP.

00:33:47.396 --> 00:33:49.325
But of course,
I actually wanna do both.

00:33:49.326 --> 00:33:52.433
For every packet, it's as if I
have two copies of this packet.

00:33:52.434 --> 00:33:55.095
One I wanna monitor and
the other I wanna forward.

00:33:55.096 --> 00:33:57.895
And in practice, I really wanna
do both and synthesize a single

00:33:57.896 --> 00:34:00.295
table of rules in the switch
that went to both.

00:34:00.296 --> 00:34:02.645
And so parallel composition
does exactly that.

00:34:02.646 --> 00:34:04.595
If you look under the hood,
it's ugly.

00:34:04.596 --> 00:34:07.475
You can think of this as sort
of an ugly cross product of all

00:34:07.476 --> 00:34:09.817
combinations of sources and
destinations and

00:34:09.818 --> 00:34:12.210
do both the counting and
the forwarding.

00:34:12.211 --> 00:34:14.140
The key point here is that
the person writing the code

00:34:14.141 --> 00:34:16.170
doesn't have to think
about gobbledygook.

00:34:16.171 --> 00:34:18.040
They just think
about monitoring and

00:34:18.041 --> 00:34:19.486
routing as independent entities.

00:34:19.487 --> 00:34:21.900
They may buy their monitoring
application from one party and

00:34:21.901 --> 00:34:23.330
write their own for the other.

00:34:23.331 --> 00:34:26.148
Doesn't matter because they
can really reason about them

00:34:26.149 --> 00:34:28.630
independently and
snap them together in this way.

00:34:28.631 --> 00:34:30.900
Okay, that's sort
of the first idea.

00:34:30.901 --> 00:34:34.152
But many things besides
monitoring wanna do more to

00:34:34.153 --> 00:34:36.400
the packet than just look at it.

00:34:36.401 --> 00:34:38.330
They actually wanna change
somehow the way the packet is

00:34:38.331 --> 00:34:40.240
handled, that gets
a bit more complicated.

00:34:40.241 --> 00:34:42.250
So just to give a simple,
concrete example,

00:34:42.251 --> 00:34:43.510
imagine you wanna
build a load balancer.

00:34:44.600 --> 00:34:47.755
And I know there's been a number
of projects here, etc.,

00:34:47.756 --> 00:34:49.370
that have done exactly that.

00:34:49.371 --> 00:34:52.030
The switches can play a role
in some primitive parts of

00:34:52.031 --> 00:34:53.020
load balancing.

00:34:53.021 --> 00:34:55.140
So a common thing when you're
building a server load balancer,

00:34:55.141 --> 00:34:57.020
you have multiple
backend servers,

00:34:57.021 --> 00:34:59.580
let's say with three different
addresses, but a single public

00:34:59.581 --> 00:35:02.930
facing address that
the clients send requests to.

00:35:02.931 --> 00:35:05.700
That box may be a load balancer
and what it's gonna do is select

00:35:05.701 --> 00:35:07.280
different subsets
of the request, ito

00:35:07.281 --> 00:35:10.560
go to different backend servers
by rewriting the destination IP

00:35:10.561 --> 00:35:13.210
address to send it to
the chosen backend server.

00:35:13.211 --> 00:35:16.000
So here you can see this is
a sort of sequential operation.

00:35:16.001 --> 00:35:18.680
One is picking the backend
server to achieve some load

00:35:18.681 --> 00:35:21.560
balancing goal among whatever
servers are up at this time,

00:35:21.561 --> 00:35:24.250
and a second to actually
route the traffic to

00:35:24.251 --> 00:35:27.840
the chosen backend server
based on its choice.

00:35:27.841 --> 00:35:29.850
And so here you could
imagine a form of,

00:35:29.851 --> 00:35:31.950
think of this as the Unix
pipe operator, right?

00:35:31.951 --> 00:35:34.000
You're essentially load
balancing first, and

00:35:34.001 --> 00:35:36.480
the output of load
balancing should affect

00:35:36.481 --> 00:35:37.960
the inputs to routing.

00:35:37.961 --> 00:35:41.165
So concrete example, you might
imagine all packets whose source

00:35:41.166 --> 00:35:43.844
IP address starts with 0 that
are going to a particular

00:35:43.845 --> 00:35:46.407
public service will go to
one of the two servers, and

00:35:46.408 --> 00:35:48.530
all who start with 1
might go to the other.

00:35:48.531 --> 00:35:51.063
This is sort of roughly
dividing in half the traffic so

00:35:51.064 --> 00:35:54.700
that half the requests go to one
server and half go to the other.

00:35:54.701 --> 00:35:57.140
Routing might be completely
unaware of that process and

00:35:57.141 --> 00:35:59.730
just be deciding what
the shortest path is to get to

00:35:59.731 --> 00:36:02.720
each of these backend servers
and forwarding the package.

00:36:02.721 --> 00:36:04.871
But again, of course,
we wanna do both,

00:36:04.872 --> 00:36:08.005
which underneath the hood if
there's only one rule table and

00:36:08.006 --> 00:36:11.017
this switch is kind of a yucky
mess of matching on packets,

00:36:11.018 --> 00:36:14.028
modifying the destination and
forwarding the packet as if

00:36:14.029 --> 00:36:16.810
the destination had already
been modified, right.

00:36:16.811 --> 00:36:18.080
Because it's essentially,

00:36:18.081 --> 00:36:20.110
smushing together two
different functions and

00:36:20.111 --> 00:36:23.240
synthesizing one that has the
effect of both done in series.

00:36:23.241 --> 00:36:26.645
So it's just sort of the two
basic composition operators.

00:36:26.646 --> 00:36:29.385
And then we did a bunch of
other things around abstracting

00:36:29.386 --> 00:36:32.240
the topology, abstractions for
monitoring the network,

00:36:32.241 --> 00:36:33.097
abstractions for

00:36:33.098 --> 00:36:36.290
updating the network that I'm
not gonna talk about here.

00:36:36.291 --> 00:36:39.830
But they all kind of are in the
same spirit of trying to find

00:36:39.831 --> 00:36:44.250
simple, reusable, and composable
abstractions for querying,

00:36:44.251 --> 00:36:47.530
computing, and updating
policies on the network.

00:36:47.531 --> 00:36:48.529
So a couple lessons here.

00:36:49.930 --> 00:36:52.220
OpenFlow was
an important first step.

00:36:52.221 --> 00:36:54.090
It generalized
the capabilities of a lot

00:36:54.091 --> 00:36:56.770
of commodity networking
devices and frankly, for me,

00:36:56.771 --> 00:36:59.060
it was hugely helpful because I
could explain it to people in

00:36:59.061 --> 00:37:01.120
programming languages without
them running away screaming.

00:37:02.180 --> 00:37:05.100
One thing I find challenging
about doing interdisciplinary

00:37:05.101 --> 00:37:08.150
work in networking is our field
is riddled with acronyms and

00:37:08.151 --> 00:37:11.660
minutia, and it often is just
very off-putting to people that

00:37:11.661 --> 00:37:14.200
actually want to think
creatively in their own field.

00:37:14.201 --> 00:37:16.266
And so having a simple
abstraction to explain to

00:37:16.267 --> 00:37:19.005
a colleague without them having
to know all the alphabet soup of

00:37:19.006 --> 00:37:20.980
networking was just
tremendously helpful.

00:37:20.981 --> 00:37:23.484
I don't think we would've
been able to do this work

00:37:23.485 --> 00:37:24.250
without that.

00:37:24.251 --> 00:37:27.355
And then, from the PL community,
we really got this sort of taste

00:37:27.356 --> 00:37:29.410
for thinking in
a compositional way.

00:37:29.411 --> 00:37:32.240
And for painstakingly thinking
really hard about what were

00:37:32.241 --> 00:37:34.530
the right, simple and
reusable abstractions, and

00:37:34.531 --> 00:37:37.290
continuing to revisit them
as the work progressed.

00:37:37.291 --> 00:37:39.740
And in fact, my colleagues did
a really nice piece of followup

00:37:39.741 --> 00:37:42.900
work called Netcat, which
recognized that this policy

00:37:42.901 --> 00:37:44.980
language that I just was
describing earlier, and

00:37:44.981 --> 00:37:47.640
the composition operators
are actually a lovely example of

00:37:47.641 --> 00:37:49.900
something called a Kleene
algebra with tests,

00:37:49.901 --> 00:37:51.270
which has a long and
storied history.

00:37:51.271 --> 00:37:52.870
And so you can actually map
everything I've just been

00:37:52.871 --> 00:37:56.030
talking about and more into this
nice mathematical construct.

00:37:56.031 --> 00:37:57.132
And it's very useful for

00:37:57.133 --> 00:38:00.126
synthesizing networks, for doing
verification on networks, for

00:38:00.127 --> 00:38:01.765
virtualizing networks,
and so on.

00:38:01.766 --> 00:38:04.876
So if you have any interest in
this space the Netcat paper from

00:38:04.877 --> 00:38:07.804
Nate Foster and Dave Walker and
Dexter Kozen and others,

00:38:07.805 --> 00:38:09.660
it's a really lovely paper.

00:38:09.661 --> 00:38:12.467
So that essentially built
on top of recognizing that

00:38:12.468 --> 00:38:15.467
the abstractions we'd already
built actually were not

00:38:15.468 --> 00:38:16.310
entirely new.

00:38:16.311 --> 00:38:18.521
They really had a very
nice mapping into

00:38:18.522 --> 00:38:20.050
some existing ideas.

00:38:20.051 --> 00:38:22.236
And as I mentioned, we also
kind of came back to that whole

00:38:22.237 --> 00:38:24.287
control loop that I've been
obsessed with ever since

00:38:24.288 --> 00:38:25.539
I started working with Albert.

00:38:25.540 --> 00:38:27.744
That we want abstractions for
measurement, for

00:38:27.745 --> 00:38:30.704
making decisions, and finally
for doing distributed updates to

00:38:30.705 --> 00:38:33.800
whole collections of switches as
if it happens in one fell swoop.

00:38:33.801 --> 00:38:37.420
And so there's a body of
work focused on that also.

00:38:37.421 --> 00:38:41.450
The human lesson here,
one was really Nate.

00:38:41.451 --> 00:38:45.050
So Nate did a postdoc with
me and Dave at Princeton for

00:38:45.051 --> 00:38:46.690
a year before he
went to Cornell.

00:38:46.691 --> 00:38:48.470
And he basically was
embedded in my group,

00:38:48.471 --> 00:38:52.040
sitting with networking people,
absorbing networking lore,

00:38:52.041 --> 00:38:54.960
writing lots of painful code on
the early OpenFlow controllers

00:38:54.961 --> 00:38:59.360
at the time, experiencing pain
and reflecting on that pain.

00:38:59.361 --> 00:39:01.640
So this was very much a learn
by doing kind of project.

00:39:01.641 --> 00:39:05.400
There was an open source network
controller called NOX that

00:39:05.401 --> 00:39:08.190
was an enabler there because
we could get started trying to

00:39:08.191 --> 00:39:09.310
build real applications.

00:39:09.311 --> 00:39:11.920
And then reflect on
what was painful,

00:39:11.921 --> 00:39:14.960
what kind of repeat code
we had in multiple places.

00:39:14.961 --> 00:39:17.780
And a lot of that allowed
the extractions to follow.

00:39:17.781 --> 00:39:20.220
And finally, there's been
a lot of work by Nate,

00:39:20.221 --> 00:39:22.470
largely by Nate, but
also to some extent by me and

00:39:22.471 --> 00:39:25.840
Dave, on creating software
that other people could use.

00:39:25.841 --> 00:39:29.050
Tutorials, summer schools,
keynote talks in each others'

00:39:29.051 --> 00:39:31.720
conferences, publishing in
each others' conferences,

00:39:31.721 --> 00:39:34.440
that I think helped a lot in
creating a community of people

00:39:34.441 --> 00:39:36.590
that were able to
build on this work.

00:39:36.591 --> 00:39:39.220
And now we see in both PL
conferences and networking

00:39:39.221 --> 00:39:42.600
conferences a significant
number of papers in both.

00:39:42.601 --> 00:39:43.580
Just as an anecdote there,

00:39:43.581 --> 00:39:47.590
the second PL paper that was
sent to POPL in this space,

00:39:47.591 --> 00:39:50.240
the reviewers came back saying
there's already been one paper

00:39:50.241 --> 00:39:52.080
on programmable networks,
aren't we done?

00:39:53.380 --> 00:39:55.118
And I think we don't
get that anymore, and

00:39:55.119 --> 00:39:57.390
I'm sure networking, now I
hear people complaining hey,

00:39:57.391 --> 00:39:59.040
a third of the conference
is on PL now.

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:03.462
So I think there's definitely
been some misunderstanding of

00:40:03.463 --> 00:40:05.200
how rich the problem
space might be.

00:40:05.201 --> 00:40:06.984
But I do think we're
sort of over a hump now,

00:40:06.985 --> 00:40:09.399
where people appreciate that
there's some really rich and

00:40:09.400 --> 00:40:12.190
interesting work to do at
the intersection, yeah?

00:40:12.191 --> 00:40:13.030
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I saw Ned

00:40:13.031 --> 00:40:13.870
talking about-
&gt;&gt; I hope you

00:40:13.871 --> 00:40:14.562
weren't that reviewer.

00:40:14.563 --> 00:40:17.750
&gt;&gt; What's that?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:17.751 --> 00:40:18.380
&gt;&gt; No, I saw Nate

00:40:18.381 --> 00:40:21.110
talk about P4 and verifying P4.

00:40:21.111 --> 00:40:23.960
I'm curious, it seems like
my understanding of sort

00:40:23.961 --> 00:40:26.390
of where this language went

00:40:26.391 --> 00:40:30.810
was it got to sorta like the C
programming level of specifying,

00:40:30.811 --> 00:40:33.250
which is a much higher level
than you started with.

00:40:33.251 --> 00:40:36.490
But it's not at the level of
sort of there's contracts and

00:40:36.491 --> 00:40:40.450
you can verify aspects of the
program statically, essentially.

00:40:40.451 --> 00:40:44.230
So I'm curious, do you think
that things went far enough, or

00:40:44.231 --> 00:40:46.440
is there more room here?

00:40:46.441 --> 00:40:50.340
Are we kind of at a point where
the network community says,

00:40:50.341 --> 00:40:52.010
yeah, this is good enough for
us?

00:40:52.011 --> 00:40:55.240
It's sort of high enough level,
or do you think there's still

00:40:55.241 --> 00:40:59.190
room for even raising the-
&gt;&gt; I completely agree that

00:40:59.191 --> 00:41:00.380
there's room to go further.

00:41:00.381 --> 00:41:02.079
Even programming in
something like frenetic,

00:41:02.080 --> 00:41:03.740
the language we created,
is quite low level.

00:41:03.741 --> 00:41:04.642
And in particular,

00:41:04.643 --> 00:41:07.189
when I talk to companies who
are not software companies of

00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:09.894
their own, not Google,
Microsoft, Facebook, and so on,

00:41:09.895 --> 00:41:12.045
they find this doesn't
solve their problem.

00:41:12.046 --> 00:41:13.980
It might be under the hood
in something that solves

00:41:13.981 --> 00:41:14.520
their problem.

00:41:14.521 --> 00:41:16.040
But it's much lower level.

00:41:16.041 --> 00:41:19.171
They wanna talk about
service level agreements and

00:41:19.172 --> 00:41:22.754
high level specifications,
exactly what you just said.

00:41:22.755 --> 00:41:25.308
So I do think there's an
exciting opportunity there that

00:41:25.309 --> 00:41:27.596
could come up with languages
that talk about that,

00:41:27.597 --> 00:41:29.404
and maybe synthesizing
under the hood,

00:41:29.405 --> 00:41:31.490
things that can be
compiled to lower levels.

00:41:31.491 --> 00:41:34.300
A second thing we really haven't
done that's really important is

00:41:34.301 --> 00:41:36.700
many of these abstractions give
a program a lot of power but

00:41:36.701 --> 00:41:38.510
not a cost model.

00:41:38.511 --> 00:41:39.807
I gave a talk on
frenetic at Google.

00:41:39.808 --> 00:41:42.056
And the first thing I heard from
the people at Google is that if

00:41:42.057 --> 00:41:44.129
you give people a programming
interface to the switch,

00:41:44.130 --> 00:41:45.465
they will bring
down your network.

00:41:45.466 --> 00:41:46.565
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, so

00:41:46.566 --> 00:41:49.324
clearly there's a need to
give people capabilities for

00:41:49.325 --> 00:41:52.154
reasoning about the overhead
of the operations they do.

00:41:52.155 --> 00:41:55.034
If you create something that
creates a cross product, and

00:41:55.035 --> 00:41:58.030
you have an explosion of state
in the switch or Create a policy

00:41:58.031 --> 00:42:01.085
that forces a lot of the packets
to go through the controller,

00:42:01.086 --> 00:42:02.060
that's not okay.

00:42:02.061 --> 00:42:04.185
And I think we're not really
still at a good point of

00:42:04.186 --> 00:42:06.808
reasoning about the overheads
that are introduced when people

00:42:06.809 --> 00:42:08.200
write these applications.

00:42:08.201 --> 00:42:10.560
And then newer switch
hardware has capabilities,

00:42:10.561 --> 00:42:11.950
I'll talk about that next,

00:42:11.951 --> 00:42:13.980
that are not captured by
this programming model.

00:42:13.981 --> 00:42:16.960
And so I think there's a lot to
do to make the underlined data

00:42:16.961 --> 00:42:18.000
plane richer and

00:42:18.001 --> 00:42:20.380
be able to put more of
the operations down there.

00:42:20.381 --> 00:42:22.560
You had something else.

00:42:22.561 --> 00:42:25.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
I just want to reflect a little

00:42:25.141 --> 00:42:26.820
bit on the lessons learned,
right?

00:42:26.821 --> 00:42:31.307
So and also about your
[INAUDIBLE] research.

00:42:31.308 --> 00:42:34.515
So I think one thing,
so in 1990, right, so

00:42:34.516 --> 00:42:38.665
in terms of separate research I
think this notion of separate in

00:42:38.666 --> 00:42:41.406
control planes with
data planes was quite

00:42:41.407 --> 00:42:44.166
well established in
wireless industry.

00:42:44.167 --> 00:42:47.069
For example this notion of
having dumb access points and

00:42:47.070 --> 00:42:49.785
smart switches was there and
then what happened is in

00:42:49.786 --> 00:42:53.010
fact I personally appear to call
architecture based on that.

00:42:53.011 --> 00:42:55.829
But what then happened is
that there was a lot of

00:42:55.830 --> 00:42:58.937
push from companies like
Microsoft itself to put,

00:42:58.938 --> 00:43:01.756
a lot of these marks in
the switches itself, so

00:43:01.757 --> 00:43:05.860
it 1x happened because they
wanted security at the switch.

00:43:05.861 --> 00:43:06.680
Now the reason for

00:43:06.681 --> 00:43:09.510
that was because there was
sort of separation of labor,

00:43:09.511 --> 00:43:10.900
like who controls what.

00:43:10.901 --> 00:43:13.080
And from a Microsoft
perspective,

00:43:13.081 --> 00:43:14.540
it was sort of
important to say well,

00:43:14.541 --> 00:43:17.600
if you take that responsibility,
I don't have to worry about it,

00:43:17.601 --> 00:43:19.070
and so
I can do the other things.

00:43:19.071 --> 00:43:20.930
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; Now what happened in the wire

00:43:20.931 --> 00:43:23.020
line networking space was,

00:43:23.021 --> 00:43:24.420
it wasn't getting
any traction either.

00:43:24.421 --> 00:43:25.900
I mean,
you guys were doing it, and

00:43:25.901 --> 00:43:27.350
Enterprise wasn't
getting any action.

00:43:27.351 --> 00:43:30.430
It was only because
Cloud became a thing,

00:43:30.431 --> 00:43:34.730
where it became that everybody
owned their own equipment.

00:43:34.731 --> 00:43:37.460
And once you own
the equipment you wanted to

00:43:37.461 --> 00:43:40.460
have the flexibility to program
your own equipment because you

00:43:40.461 --> 00:43:43.180
were being sold features that
you didn't really care about,

00:43:43.181 --> 00:43:45.410
and you were spending a lot
of money for that stuff.

00:43:45.411 --> 00:43:48.060
So even though this is
happening in academia,

00:43:48.061 --> 00:43:50.750
what is happening in industry,
in the business is actually

00:43:50.751 --> 00:43:53.890
impacting how effective
any of this is becoming.

00:43:53.891 --> 00:43:57.990
And so I think as you reflect on
all these things and as we move

00:43:57.991 --> 00:44:01.770
forward, I think that has to be
a pretty serious consideration

00:44:01.771 --> 00:44:03.840
about how businesses
are moving forward and

00:44:03.841 --> 00:44:06.510
what is happening there,
why research has been done.

00:44:06.511 --> 00:44:08.510
Otherwise, you do
decades of research and

00:44:08.511 --> 00:44:10.550
nobody ever pays
attention to it.

00:44:10.551 --> 00:44:12.680
And I think when that happens,

00:44:12.681 --> 00:44:13.930
then these things
start to light up.

00:44:13.931 --> 00:44:16.576
Even now, for example, if you
look at the internet in wide

00:44:16.577 --> 00:44:18.632
area, these things
are not taken out there.

00:44:18.633 --> 00:44:22.386
So the question that Erik was
asking about the economics of

00:44:22.387 --> 00:44:24.911
it, I think that is
more spot on because

00:44:24.912 --> 00:44:27.899
the economics drives
that design as opposed to

00:44:27.900 --> 00:44:30.600
enjoyability like [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah I that though

00:44:30.601 --> 00:44:33.319
kinda what you said particularly
just to put it in the context of

00:44:33.320 --> 00:44:34.140
OpenFlow I think so

00:44:34.141 --> 00:44:36.552
there's a whole body of
people in networking,

00:44:36.553 --> 00:44:39.272
there's a whole body of work in
active networking that didn't

00:44:39.273 --> 00:44:41.969
get off the ground sort of in
the mid 90s, mid or late 90s.

00:44:41.970 --> 00:44:43.922
And I think what happened
with OpenFlow that

00:44:43.923 --> 00:44:46.416
made it more successful was
partially timing, right.

00:44:46.417 --> 00:44:49.090
There were starting to be third
party vendors merchant silicon

00:44:49.091 --> 00:44:51.770
companies that were selling
chipsets for building switches.

00:44:51.771 --> 00:44:54.186
So you could build a switch
without being a large vendor

00:44:54.187 --> 00:44:56.602
with its own fabrication
facility and there were cloud

00:44:56.603 --> 00:44:58.860
operators who had unique
requirements and ability

00:44:58.861 --> 00:45:01.190
to write on code and that
was sort of a perfect storm.

00:45:01.191 --> 00:45:02.760
And I think what Nick McKeown,
in particular,

00:45:02.761 --> 00:45:04.320
because he was really the one
who spearheaded the work

00:45:04.321 --> 00:45:06.830
on OpenFlow, was to recognize
this was a special moment.

00:45:06.831 --> 00:45:07.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:45:07.561 --> 00:45:09.630
&gt;&gt; And that if some effort
was put in place at the right

00:45:09.631 --> 00:45:12.930
time to get the APIs in place, a
lot of good stuff would follow.

00:45:12.931 --> 00:45:15.940
And so I do think that
the definition of OpenFlow was

00:45:15.941 --> 00:45:19.020
strategic, it's not
a beautiful spec.

00:45:19.021 --> 00:45:21.930
But it was at the right time,
and had a right sweet spot of

00:45:21.931 --> 00:45:24.360
being flexible enough to
do something interesting,

00:45:24.361 --> 00:45:26.710
without being ahead of what the
hardware could do at the time.

00:45:26.711 --> 00:45:28.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I just wanted to have

00:45:29.430 --> 00:45:32.310
acknowledgement of the fact
that a lot of things that

00:45:32.311 --> 00:45:33.850
are external to
the community happened,

00:45:33.851 --> 00:45:36.400
which caused something to-
&gt;&gt; Completely agree.

00:45:36.401 --> 00:45:39.190
&gt;&gt; Otherwise, you're just
can't be doing great work,

00:45:39.191 --> 00:45:40.250
it doesn't really do anything.

00:45:40.251 --> 00:45:41.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah and I guess I sort of

00:45:41.011 --> 00:45:42.380
hinted a bit at that
in the first project,

00:45:42.381 --> 00:45:44.990
which was not successful at the
time in terms of give you use

00:45:44.991 --> 00:45:47.770
success in terms of having
successful tech transfer.

00:45:47.771 --> 00:45:50.795
Because it was sort of in that
sense ahead in that it assumed

00:45:50.796 --> 00:45:54.007
capabilities of the hardware
that didn't exist at the time,

00:45:54.008 --> 00:45:55.242
so there was nowhere for

00:45:55.243 --> 00:45:57.367
it to go when the work
was actually done.

00:45:57.368 --> 00:45:58.254
Yeah.

00:45:58.255 --> 00:46:00.984
&gt;&gt; I have a more technical
question, but I think you've

00:46:00.985 --> 00:46:04.175
partially answered it when you
commented about cost models.

00:46:04.176 --> 00:46:04.890
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:46:04.891 --> 00:46:08.090
&gt;&gt; Because in these
SDS situations,

00:46:08.091 --> 00:46:11.450
it's not just all about
composition modularity, even for

00:46:11.451 --> 00:46:13.400
the examples you gave
on both balancing and

00:46:13.401 --> 00:46:16.930
NAT, you have to do at least
some arithmetic minimally.

00:46:18.570 --> 00:46:21.931
So what are the limits on,
what, can you

00:46:21.932 --> 00:46:26.460
write [INAUDIBLE] complete
computations, for example?

00:46:26.461 --> 00:46:28.887
&gt;&gt; So one way to think about it
is that the underlying switch

00:46:28.888 --> 00:46:31.317
has this very limited match
action paradigm that's not

00:46:31.318 --> 00:46:34.005
remotely close to [INAUDIBLE]
complete and then the controller

00:46:34.006 --> 00:46:36.860
is an arbitrary software
that can do everything else.

00:46:36.861 --> 00:46:37.900
Of course that's a glib

00:46:37.901 --> 00:46:39.940
statement because of
then at great cost.

00:46:39.941 --> 00:46:43.020
Right, so they're sort of
similar to wireless setting that

00:46:43.021 --> 00:46:43.942
Victor was talking about,

00:46:43.943 --> 00:46:47.330
you essentially have these dumb
switch devices that are fast

00:46:47.331 --> 00:46:49.880
but only have relatively simple
computational model then we got

00:46:49.881 --> 00:46:53.420
the slow central control that
can do just about anything.

00:46:53.421 --> 00:46:55.430
And what's happened in the last
few years is a revisiting of

00:46:55.431 --> 00:46:57.590
what the switch could do,
if you're willing to now

00:46:57.591 --> 00:47:00.330
say programmable networks of
what I want, and I'm willing to

00:47:00.331 --> 00:47:02.350
design new switches with
that paradigm in mind,

00:47:02.351 --> 00:47:04.720
then there is an opportunity
to think about what can go in

00:47:04.721 --> 00:47:06.780
the switches to make
it possible but

00:47:06.781 --> 00:47:08.980
do more of the computation
directly in the switch.

00:47:08.981 --> 00:47:11.997
&gt;&gt; So is it reasonable to guess,
on this specific work,

00:47:11.998 --> 00:47:15.552
that you're talking about,
especially with David Walker and

00:47:15.553 --> 00:47:18.974
Nate Foster involved, these
fairly powerful functional,

00:47:18.975 --> 00:47:21.813
do you have the whole
lambda calculus available?

00:47:21.814 --> 00:47:24.458
&gt;&gt; So you could in fact you can
essentially view that really

00:47:24.459 --> 00:47:27.101
what you're doing is installing
a set of functions, or

00:47:27.102 --> 00:47:29.970
a composed set of functions
on the network over time.

00:47:29.971 --> 00:47:30.740
&gt;&gt; I see.
&gt;&gt; And

00:47:30.741 --> 00:47:32.660
the software that decides
what the functions

00:47:32.661 --> 00:47:35.590
should be at any given time
should be arbitrary code right.

00:47:35.591 --> 00:47:38.550
And Nate had a version of
Frenetic that was implemented

00:47:38.551 --> 00:47:41.110
in OCamel we had a version
implemented in Python, but

00:47:41.111 --> 00:47:43.040
essentially you could
imagine taking whatever

00:47:43.041 --> 00:47:44.850
other programming constructs
you might want for

00:47:44.851 --> 00:47:47.720
constructing those functions and
then across apps you

00:47:47.721 --> 00:47:50.740
would compose them and
synthesize them.

00:47:50.741 --> 00:47:55.000
&gt;&gt; Then I guess Victor's earlier
comments tie into this because

00:47:55.001 --> 00:47:57.870
partly what the equipment
manufacturers are willing to

00:47:57.871 --> 00:48:02.405
actually put in to
the underlying network

00:48:02.406 --> 00:48:04.970
infrastructure would
also put a limit to

00:48:06.090 --> 00:48:07.450
what's actually practical then.

00:48:07.451 --> 00:48:09.763
&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly and OpenFlow
is embarrassingly cognisant of

00:48:09.764 --> 00:48:12.120
that, right, I mean OpenFlow
basically defined only things

00:48:12.121 --> 00:48:14.520
that could be done in
existing switch hardware.

00:48:14.521 --> 00:48:16.710
And I try to be as general as I
could subject to that constraint

00:48:16.711 --> 00:48:18.580
but that's a fairly
significant constraint.

00:48:18.581 --> 00:48:21.240
And to the question that you
asked about a few minutes

00:48:21.241 --> 00:48:24.010
ago, the software running above
the switch is now kind of

00:48:24.011 --> 00:48:24.830
arbitrary code.

00:48:24.831 --> 00:48:27.020
So if you wanna think
about verification,

00:48:27.021 --> 00:48:28.010
that's not good, right?

00:48:28.011 --> 00:48:30.410
So you definitely want languages
further up the stack if you

00:48:30.411 --> 00:48:32.460
wanna be able to reason
collectively about the entire

00:48:32.461 --> 00:48:35.230
system, not just about the part
that's running in the network.

00:48:35.231 --> 00:48:38.150
&gt;&gt; Jen, just about the open.

00:48:38.151 --> 00:48:41.490
The original spec
was pretty because

00:48:41.491 --> 00:48:43.088
you guys try to be very general,
right?

00:48:43.089 --> 00:48:46.770
So it was very bloated in a way
that the switch manufacturers

00:48:46.771 --> 00:48:49.580
didn't actually commit
the entire spec, right?

00:48:49.581 --> 00:48:52.820
So when we talk to a lot
of these guys, we implement

00:48:52.821 --> 00:48:55.741
some subset of these things
as Peter was saying.

00:48:55.742 --> 00:48:58.481
So I guess the question is that
would you reflect back and

00:48:58.482 --> 00:49:00.929
say that maybe you were too
general in some sense or

00:49:00.930 --> 00:49:03.627
trying to solve too many
problems for too many people?

00:49:03.628 --> 00:49:05.840
&gt;&gt; That's a really
good question.

00:49:05.841 --> 00:49:07.496
So the Open Flow spec evolved.

00:49:07.497 --> 00:49:09.750
There's sorta OpenFlow 1.0
that was pretty simple and

00:49:09.751 --> 00:49:12.970
then it quickly became 1.1,
1.2 and so on.

00:49:12.971 --> 00:49:15.336
And I think what happened
is people wanted greater

00:49:15.337 --> 00:49:18.377
expressiveness in the switch,
and yet, the more that happened

00:49:18.378 --> 00:49:21.215
the more complex the spec got,
it became the kitchen sink.

00:49:21.216 --> 00:49:24.284
The original OpenFlow spec could
match on like 12 header fields

00:49:24.285 --> 00:49:27.239
with a simple set of actions,
1.5 could match on 42 header

00:49:27.240 --> 00:49:29.805
fields and do a whole bunch
other actions with multiple

00:49:29.806 --> 00:49:32.490
stages of tables and
your brain starts to explode.

00:49:32.491 --> 00:49:34.090
And some vendors
implement some parts, and

00:49:34.091 --> 00:49:35.180
some implement the other.

00:49:35.181 --> 00:49:36.805
&gt;&gt; So
what do you take back on that,

00:49:36.806 --> 00:49:38.490
cuz we saw that
in very real way.

00:49:38.491 --> 00:49:41.420
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no I think the industry
got ahead of itself.

00:49:41.421 --> 00:49:43.595
It's understandable
because people wanted to

00:49:43.596 --> 00:49:46.368
write applications that couldn't
run efficiently running

00:49:46.369 --> 00:49:47.250
OpenFlow 1.0.

00:49:47.251 --> 00:49:49.180
So that's this sort of
other side of the coin of

00:49:49.181 --> 00:49:51.890
being pragmatic to the existing
switch hardware and

00:49:51.891 --> 00:49:55.200
I think that's why you're
seeing now new switch hardware,

00:49:55.201 --> 00:49:57.170
right, that's trying to
address the limitation

00:49:57.171 --> 00:49:59.530
of functionality that-
&gt;&gt; All of these questions

00:49:59.531 --> 00:50:02.980
matter a lot to the networks
firm, the clouds also.

00:50:02.981 --> 00:50:03.500
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:50:03.501 --> 00:50:04.001
Yeah.

00:50:05.060 --> 00:50:05.950
Exactly.

00:50:05.951 --> 00:50:08.534
And I think many people found
OpenFlow was too much and

00:50:08.535 --> 00:50:09.575
not enough, right.

00:50:09.576 --> 00:50:12.491
And so a lot of people started
doing things in the hypervisor

00:50:12.492 --> 00:50:15.465
or in the nick because OpenFlow
switch is more capable enough

00:50:15.466 --> 00:50:16.065
to do more.

00:50:16.066 --> 00:50:18.246
And some of that's just because
they were defined that way

00:50:18.247 --> 00:50:19.370
because of the legacy chips.

00:50:19.371 --> 00:50:22.488
And some of it is, it's really
at line rate in this wedge to do

00:50:22.489 --> 00:50:24.393
really complicated things, yeah.

00:50:24.394 --> 00:50:25.840
So I thing the vote's still out,
actually, right?

00:50:25.841 --> 00:50:27.730
In fact, to Ben's question
about programming,

00:50:27.731 --> 00:50:29.800
I think many people don't
wanna program the network.

00:50:29.801 --> 00:50:31.470
They wanna program the system.

00:50:31.471 --> 00:50:34.290
And a lot of this work is still
focused on the network, right?

00:50:34.291 --> 00:50:36.983
And you'd like to think you're
gonna talk about the machines

00:50:36.984 --> 00:50:38.031
as a pool of resources.

00:50:38.032 --> 00:50:41.840
You know, the servers, the
nicks, the switches, and so on.

00:50:41.841 --> 00:50:43.279
And right now it's
still very siloed.

00:50:43.280 --> 00:50:44.542
So I think there's
a lot of scope to do.

00:50:44.543 --> 00:50:47.670
Not only to go up the stack,
but to go broader, as well.

00:50:48.850 --> 00:50:52.780
So that's a great segue
to thinking about

00:50:52.781 --> 00:50:55.220
the ability to
program new hardware.

00:50:55.221 --> 00:50:57.340
So the last thing I
wanted to talk about was

00:50:57.341 --> 00:50:58.180
some really recent work.

00:50:58.181 --> 00:50:59.860
So this is gonna be a little
bit more narrow and

00:50:59.861 --> 00:51:01.985
a little more focused on a very
specific technical problem,

00:51:01.986 --> 00:51:05.235
just to illustrate some of
the work we've been doing on

00:51:05.236 --> 00:51:06.435
network monitoring.

00:51:06.436 --> 00:51:09.035
So the big frustration in a lot
of the work that I was involved

00:51:09.036 --> 00:51:12.055
in at AT&amp;T is many networking
problems, knowing what's going

00:51:12.056 --> 00:51:14.492
on is harder than knowing
what to do about it.

00:51:14.493 --> 00:51:16.182
And that's often because we
just don't have sufficient

00:51:16.183 --> 00:51:19.162
visibility into the traffic,
the performance, and so on.

00:51:19.163 --> 00:51:21.932
Often working with measurement
techniques that are quite coarse

00:51:21.933 --> 00:51:23.442
grained and slow.

00:51:23.443 --> 00:51:26.000
So I became very interested
in how to leverage emerging

00:51:26.001 --> 00:51:28.568
switch hardware to be able
to do better measurements.

00:51:28.569 --> 00:51:31.242
And that's sort of the big
focus on my current research.

00:51:31.243 --> 00:51:33.632
So the work on Openflow and
Frenetic and so

00:51:33.633 --> 00:51:36.653
on also led to a bunch of
programming obstructions.

00:51:36.654 --> 00:51:39.447
That led us to rethink what
we would want the hardware of

00:51:39.448 --> 00:51:42.255
the future to look like if
we could go beyond Openflow.

00:51:42.256 --> 00:51:44.597
And so a number of people
became interested, us and

00:51:44.598 --> 00:51:47.485
other groups as well, in more
programmable packet processing

00:51:47.486 --> 00:51:48.870
directly in the switch.

00:51:48.871 --> 00:51:51.050
And so I was gonna tell you
a little bit about how current

00:51:51.051 --> 00:51:53.990
switch hardware that's coming
out of a number of companies,

00:51:53.991 --> 00:51:57.070
Barefoot Networks, NIC cards
from Netronome, and Xilinx, and

00:51:57.071 --> 00:52:00.010
so on, how they're thinking
about what the switch hardware

00:52:00.011 --> 00:52:01.320
of the future should do.

00:52:01.321 --> 00:52:04.086
The first is that instead of
having a fixed set of header

00:52:04.087 --> 00:52:07.697
fields, first 12, then a bunch
more, then 41, then 42 in later

00:52:07.698 --> 00:52:10.478
versions of Open Flow,
there should just be a parser.

00:52:10.479 --> 00:52:13.078
And that parser should be
programmable as sort of a little

00:52:13.079 --> 00:52:15.568
state machine that can decide
how to read the packet and

00:52:15.569 --> 00:52:17.760
which fields to extract from it.

00:52:17.761 --> 00:52:18.797
This is not
a particularly deep idea.

00:52:18.798 --> 00:52:20.510
And in fact a lot of
switch hardware has this.

00:52:20.511 --> 00:52:22.709
It's just that it's not
exposed to the programmer.

00:52:22.710 --> 00:52:25.200
So a programable parser.

00:52:25.201 --> 00:52:27.203
And then a series of
match-action tables,

00:52:27.204 --> 00:52:28.500
just like Openflow had, but

00:52:28.501 --> 00:52:31.274
able to match on the fields that
are coming out of the parser.

00:52:31.275 --> 00:52:34.113
And also on metadata that might
get passed along from one stage

00:52:34.114 --> 00:52:35.310
to the next.

00:52:35.311 --> 00:52:37.220
Now often the computation
you wanna do might

00:52:37.221 --> 00:52:38.998
go across multiple packets.

00:52:38.999 --> 00:52:41.600
And so it'd be nice to have
some storage, some registers,

00:52:41.601 --> 00:52:45.100
if you will, that can keep
state about history of past

00:52:45.101 --> 00:52:48.520
packets and computations
on them in the switch.

00:52:48.521 --> 00:52:50.293
And then ability to pass state,
also,

00:52:50.294 --> 00:52:52.005
from one stage to the next.

00:52:52.006 --> 00:52:54.850
So that some of the two big
things here are being able to

00:52:54.851 --> 00:52:55.810
parse, match, and

00:52:55.811 --> 00:52:58.540
act on a programmable
set of header fields.

00:52:58.541 --> 00:53:00.846
And the second is the ability
to create a maintained state.

00:53:00.847 --> 00:53:03.813
State across stages,
between stages in the switch.

00:53:03.814 --> 00:53:06.677
And state and registers that
might be read when the next

00:53:06.678 --> 00:53:07.804
packet comes along.

00:53:07.805 --> 00:53:11.020
So this computational model
is still pretty primitive.

00:53:11.021 --> 00:53:12.970
But it's much richer
than Openflow.

00:53:12.971 --> 00:53:14.830
And it's still possible
to run at line rate.

00:53:14.831 --> 00:53:17.460
So again, the goal here
is to find the sweet spot

00:53:17.461 --> 00:53:20.040
that's amenable to efficient
hardware implementation

00:53:20.041 --> 00:53:21.140
to run at line rate.

00:53:21.141 --> 00:53:23.660
And yet still as programmable
as we can make it,

00:53:23.661 --> 00:53:25.210
subject to that constraint.

00:53:25.211 --> 00:53:27.550
And so the problem space I'm
particularly interested in here

00:53:27.551 --> 00:53:28.910
is measurement.

00:53:28.911 --> 00:53:31.480
So what we have here in
this picture is a streaming

00:53:31.481 --> 00:53:32.480
platform, right?

00:53:32.481 --> 00:53:33.953
We've got a modest
amount of state.

00:53:33.954 --> 00:53:35.953
Modest computational
capabilities.

00:53:35.954 --> 00:53:39.054
And a pipelined architecture
that one can do processing in

00:53:39.055 --> 00:53:40.330
multiple stages.

00:53:40.331 --> 00:53:43.246
It's a perfect fit for the
problem of streaming algorithms,

00:53:43.247 --> 00:53:46.048
were you wanna do some simple
computation on packets as they

00:53:46.049 --> 00:53:48.023
fly by,
keep a limited amount of states.

00:53:48.024 --> 00:53:50.732
And there's actually a very
rich literature in theoretical

00:53:50.733 --> 00:53:53.280
computer science that goes
under the name of compact data

00:53:53.281 --> 00:53:55.671
structures or streaming
algorithms, or sketches,

00:53:55.672 --> 00:53:58.275
that create a great opportunity
to actually realize those

00:53:58.276 --> 00:54:01.150
algorithms now,
directly in the data plane.

00:54:01.151 --> 00:54:02.307
But there's a catch.

00:54:02.308 --> 00:54:04.120
The theory literature
is not a perfect fit,

00:54:04.121 --> 00:54:06.619
because they often assume a
richer computational model than

00:54:06.620 --> 00:54:08.210
the one I just presented.

00:54:08.211 --> 00:54:11.212
And so, a lot of this work
is taking ideas from compact

00:54:11.213 --> 00:54:12.292
data structures.

00:54:12.293 --> 00:54:14.182
And figuring out where
it doesn't work because

00:54:14.183 --> 00:54:16.639
it violates some assumption of
the computational model I just

00:54:16.640 --> 00:54:17.171
presented.

00:54:17.172 --> 00:54:20.085
And trying to find a meeting of
the minds, where hopefully we

00:54:20.086 --> 00:54:22.943
can approximate those algorithms
in some reasonable way,

00:54:22.944 --> 00:54:25.320
while still working
within the switch.

00:54:25.321 --> 00:54:27.578
In particular, we've got a
pipelined model of computation.

00:54:27.579 --> 00:54:30.362
It's very difficult,
after you've read a register or

00:54:30.363 --> 00:54:33.276
written it, to come back to it
later with the same packet.

00:54:33.277 --> 00:54:36.656
You sort of have to leave behind
you something lying in wait for

00:54:36.657 --> 00:54:37.635
the next packet.

00:54:37.636 --> 00:54:41.410
But it's very hard to do repeat
operations on the same data.

00:54:41.411 --> 00:54:44.319
The amount of memory involved
is quite modest, and

00:54:44.320 --> 00:54:48.054
there's simple AOU's associated
with the action processing.

00:54:48.055 --> 00:54:52.629
But you can do very simple if,
then, else processing, addition,

00:54:52.630 --> 00:54:55.137
subtraction, not division, etc.

00:54:55.138 --> 00:54:58.172
So again, a very very
primitive computational model.

00:54:58.173 --> 00:55:01.055
So the question we looked at
was sort of very natural one to

00:55:01.056 --> 00:55:04.280
think about,
which is heavy-hitter detection.

00:55:04.281 --> 00:55:06.850
In my network there might be
tens of thousands of flows going

00:55:06.851 --> 00:55:09.410
through it, all conversations
taking place independently.

00:55:09.411 --> 00:55:11.240
I wanna know the k largest.

00:55:11.241 --> 00:55:15.250
Or the ones that are exceeding
a particular rate of sending.

00:55:15.251 --> 00:55:16.150
And the challenge is

00:55:16.151 --> 00:55:18.120
I don't have memory to store
data amount of reflow.

00:55:19.180 --> 00:55:21.790
So in the theory literature
there's a simple algorithm.

00:55:21.791 --> 00:55:23.217
It's a very natural
algorithm for

00:55:23.218 --> 00:55:25.340
doing this called
the Space-Savings algorithm.

00:55:25.341 --> 00:55:28.100
Essentially what you do is
you store a key-value table.

00:55:28.101 --> 00:55:29.803
The key is, let's say,
an identifier for the flow.

00:55:29.804 --> 00:55:31.491
This could be the source
IP address or

00:55:31.492 --> 00:55:32.913
the source destination pair.

00:55:32.914 --> 00:55:35.630
Or whatever you
define a flow as.

00:55:35.631 --> 00:55:38.190
And the value is a count
of the number of packets or

00:55:38.191 --> 00:55:40.690
the number of bytes that have
been seen for this flow so far.

00:55:40.691 --> 00:55:43.910
And the goal is to identify
only those above a threshold or

00:55:43.911 --> 00:55:46.670
only those that are the biggest.

00:55:46.671 --> 00:55:48.170
And so the basic idea
of the Space-Savings

00:55:48.171 --> 00:55:50.570
algorithm is if a key comes in
that's already in the table,

00:55:50.571 --> 00:55:52.270
you just increment its count.

00:55:52.271 --> 00:55:55.410
If the table's not full and a
new key comes in, you put it in.

00:55:55.411 --> 00:55:58.512
And if the table is full, and
you have to evict something,

00:55:58.513 --> 00:56:00.442
you evict,
what would you expect?

00:56:00.443 --> 00:56:03.150
You would evict the key with
the minimum value, right?

00:56:03.151 --> 00:56:05.730
Because that's the one least
likely to be significant

00:56:05.731 --> 00:56:06.640
down the road.

00:56:06.641 --> 00:56:08.603
And so, you would find
key 5 with a value of 1.

00:56:08.604 --> 00:56:11.150
And you would evict it
to make room for key 7.

00:56:11.151 --> 00:56:13.870
That's it, yeah, that's sort
of the basic algorithm.

00:56:13.871 --> 00:56:15.880
This does not work in
the computational model I

00:56:15.881 --> 00:56:17.060
mentioned for
a bunch of reasons.

00:56:17.061 --> 00:56:20.430
First of all, it requires an
order and scan at the table to

00:56:20.431 --> 00:56:23.660
find the current minimum,
which isn't gonna be feasible.

00:56:23.661 --> 00:56:26.309
We really wanna do one read or
a small number of reads and

00:56:26.310 --> 00:56:28.568
writes in each stage of
the table processing.

00:56:28.569 --> 00:56:30.058
So we can't do this.

00:56:30.059 --> 00:56:31.101
So we're just gonna walk

00:56:31.102 --> 00:56:33.045
you through succession
of some simple ideas.

00:56:33.046 --> 00:56:33.960
They're gonna each be natural.

00:56:33.961 --> 00:56:37.228
But at the end they turn into a
solution that can run In one of

00:56:37.229 --> 00:56:38.240
these switches.

00:56:38.241 --> 00:56:41.170
So the first idea, very
simple one, is that you would

00:56:41.171 --> 00:56:43.600
not need to look at the entire
table to find the true minimum.

00:56:43.601 --> 00:56:44.950
If you got kinda
close to the minimum,

00:56:44.951 --> 00:56:46.470
that would probably
be good enough.

00:56:46.471 --> 00:56:48.740
So let's look at d
entries in the table,

00:56:48.741 --> 00:56:50.322
rather than all n of them.

00:56:50.323 --> 00:56:52.080
Now just a simple case,
let's suppose it's 2.

00:56:52.081 --> 00:56:53.580
Then when the key comes in,

00:56:53.581 --> 00:56:56.140
you might apply two hash
functions to the key.

00:56:56.141 --> 00:56:57.806
Look at two different
locations in the memory.

00:56:57.807 --> 00:57:00.252
And recognize, well one of these
is the smaller of the two.

00:57:00.253 --> 00:57:01.010
I'll evict that.

00:57:01.011 --> 00:57:02.114
Its not perfect.

00:57:02.115 --> 00:57:03.819
I would have loved to
have evicted key 5.

00:57:03.820 --> 00:57:06.075
But I didn't know about it cuz
I happened not to pick it.

00:57:06.076 --> 00:57:08.976
But the hope is that you would
do a reasonably good job not

00:57:08.977 --> 00:57:11.076
evicting one of
the true heavy hitters,

00:57:11.077 --> 00:57:13.445
if you apply the technique
like that, okay?

00:57:13.446 --> 00:57:14.841
Now even that is not ideal,

00:57:14.842 --> 00:57:17.880
because it does multiple
memory accesses at a time.

00:57:17.881 --> 00:57:20.818
So a simple extension of this
idea is to make this table.

00:57:20.819 --> 00:57:23.047
Actually multiple tables,
one per stage,

00:57:23.048 --> 00:57:25.100
in the pipeline of processing.

00:57:25.101 --> 00:57:27.500
So a packet would now
have a single read and

00:57:27.501 --> 00:57:30.490
write in a single
entry in each stage.

00:57:30.491 --> 00:57:33.987
So you might, let's say,
hash the key here, and

00:57:33.988 --> 00:57:36.915
recognize that key 2
should be evicted.

00:57:36.916 --> 00:57:37.995
We have a problem, though.

00:57:37.996 --> 00:57:40.815
Cuz if I were to go through
multiple stages of processing

00:57:40.816 --> 00:57:43.815
and look at key 2 and key 4,
and then compute the minimum,

00:57:43.816 --> 00:57:46.515
I don't have the luxury of
circulating back to update

00:57:46.516 --> 00:57:48.920
the table entry that has
the true minimum in it.

00:57:48.921 --> 00:57:51.540
I only know the minimum
of these d things

00:57:51.541 --> 00:57:53.600
when I get to the end
of the processing.

00:57:53.601 --> 00:57:55.480
And I don't have the luxury,
without great efficiency,

00:57:55.481 --> 00:57:58.390
to route the packet back through
again a second time to be able

00:57:58.391 --> 00:58:01.070
to actually do the updates
to evict the true minimum.

00:58:01.071 --> 00:58:03.280
I don't know at the beginning
this is actually be the minimum,

00:58:03.281 --> 00:58:05.970
because I haven't looked
at the rest of the tables.

00:58:05.971 --> 00:58:08.852
So the final solution we came
up with essentially does

00:58:08.853 --> 00:58:11.736
the computation of the minimum
in a rolling fashion,

00:58:11.737 --> 00:58:15.018
along the pipeline, rather
than doing any recirculation.

00:58:15.019 --> 00:58:18.380
And the basic idea is this,
I come in with a key.

00:58:18.381 --> 00:58:20.300
I look, I evict the first entry.

00:58:20.301 --> 00:58:21.780
Gee, that was a huge mistake.

00:58:21.781 --> 00:58:23.855
That's actually the biggest
flow in the system.

00:58:23.856 --> 00:58:24.967
So I really don't
wanna evict it.

00:58:24.968 --> 00:58:25.939
But that's okay.

00:58:25.940 --> 00:58:29.289
I'm gonna carry it along to the
next stage where it has a chance

00:58:29.290 --> 00:58:31.745
to compete with whatever
entry it hashes to.

00:58:31.746 --> 00:58:33.553
And it'll compete now and
recognize,

00:58:33.554 --> 00:58:35.704
well I'm bigger than key
4 with a value of 2.

00:58:35.705 --> 00:58:37.654
And so that's the one
that gets evicted.

00:58:37.655 --> 00:58:40.881
So essentially by processing
each packet once in each stage,

00:58:40.882 --> 00:58:43.500
reading and
writing one entry at each stage.

00:58:43.501 --> 00:58:46.120
And passing a little metadata
from one step to the next

00:58:46.121 --> 00:58:49.760
can essentially evict something
close to the true minimum even

00:58:49.761 --> 00:58:51.580
though it's not exactly right.

00:58:51.581 --> 00:58:54.200
So just to think about how this
matches the computational model

00:58:54.201 --> 00:58:57.630
I talked about earlier, we're
essentially storing these tables

00:58:57.631 --> 00:59:00.120
in the register arrays
that I showed you earlier.

00:59:00.121 --> 00:59:01.780
We're using a simple
arithmetic logic and

00:59:01.781 --> 00:59:03.995
you have to do hash function
on the packet header.

00:59:03.996 --> 00:59:07.440
We're using simple logic to do
conditional updates to compute

00:59:07.441 --> 00:59:08.224
the minimum.

00:59:08.225 --> 00:59:10.815
And we're using small amount
of metadata to pass along

00:59:10.816 --> 00:59:13.743
with the real packet information
about its key, or about a key

00:59:13.744 --> 00:59:16.504
that's been evicted in a
previous stage in processing for

00:59:16.505 --> 00:59:17.970
this packet.

00:59:17.971 --> 00:59:20.520
And then the nice thing is that
even though this is not exactly

00:59:20.521 --> 00:59:22.992
the space savings algorithm
from the theory literature.

00:59:22.993 --> 00:59:26.040
In practice we do really close
in terms of the overhead and

00:59:26.041 --> 00:59:28.470
performance that one would
get for using that algorithm.

00:59:29.580 --> 00:59:32.150
So that's sort of a little deep
dive into one example problem.

00:59:32.151 --> 00:59:35.380
What we'd really like to do is
come up with a more general

00:59:35.381 --> 00:59:37.290
way of thinking about this
computational model and

00:59:37.291 --> 00:59:40.000
what kinds of questions can be
answered efficiently using it.

00:59:40.001 --> 00:59:42.630
But in terms of the lessons
here one is just getting

00:59:42.631 --> 00:59:44.410
at the beginning,
super concrete.

00:59:44.411 --> 00:59:46.530
Defining a very simple
computational model,

00:59:46.531 --> 00:59:49.210
inspired by the capabilities
of new hardware.

00:59:49.211 --> 00:59:51.870
A simple example problem that
has a straw man solution that's

00:59:51.871 --> 00:59:53.960
been heavily studied in
the theory literature.

00:59:53.961 --> 00:59:56.420
And then iteratively
relaxing that design

00:59:56.421 --> 00:59:59.030
based on the constraints
that the hardware imposes.

00:59:59.031 --> 01:00:00.209
But of course,
we have a long way to go.

01:00:00.210 --> 01:00:02.513
We'd like to have provable
bounds on accuracy,

01:00:02.514 --> 01:00:05.163
that's something we lack for
this solution right now.

01:00:05.164 --> 01:00:07.704
We have some sort of simple
analysis that helps us get

01:00:07.705 --> 01:00:10.850
insight, but it's not a very
tight bound on performance.

01:00:10.851 --> 01:00:12.620
And would like to have a more
general way to approach

01:00:12.621 --> 01:00:15.100
a broader set of
analysis questions

01:00:15.101 --> 01:00:16.990
using techniques of this type.

01:00:16.991 --> 01:00:18.254
So that's sort of
the third piece of work.

01:00:18.255 --> 01:00:20.608
And I think I'm just gonna end
with a few minutes of thoughts

01:00:20.609 --> 01:00:21.938
about interdisciplinary work.

01:00:21.939 --> 01:00:24.914
And when I first gave this talk
for the Athena Lecture was to

01:00:24.915 --> 01:00:27.831
a largely academic community
where I think I was trying to

01:00:27.832 --> 01:00:30.660
sell people on the merits
of interdisciplinary work.

01:00:30.661 --> 01:00:33.020
I'm at a place here where you
guys do that all the time.

01:00:33.021 --> 01:00:35.940
So indulge me for a moment on
a few thoughts about lessons

01:00:35.941 --> 01:00:39.390
about that I know I'm sort
of preaching to the choir.

01:00:39.391 --> 01:00:41.520
So I personally find
interdisciplinary work

01:00:41.521 --> 01:00:43.060
intellectually really exciting.

01:00:43.061 --> 01:00:44.959
You learn about other areas.

01:00:44.960 --> 01:00:46.544
And perhaps the more
surprising thing is you learn

01:00:46.545 --> 01:00:47.900
about your own area.

01:00:47.901 --> 01:00:49.370
When you have to explain
it to someone who

01:00:49.371 --> 01:00:51.260
is a natural skeptic and
maybe not naturally

01:00:51.261 --> 01:00:53.570
passionate about
the minutiae of your field.

01:00:53.571 --> 01:00:55.890
And so it becomes a nice way to
learn how to articulate your own

01:00:55.891 --> 01:00:57.340
field to other people and

01:00:57.341 --> 01:00:59.675
often real problems are
inherently interdisciplinary.

01:00:59.676 --> 01:01:03.080
So it's part of a broader
hope for impact on

01:01:03.081 --> 01:01:06.960
the field of IT in general,
it's a nice style of work to do.

01:01:06.961 --> 01:01:08.570
And frankly,
it's actually just a lot of fun.

01:01:08.571 --> 01:01:10.400
I've gotten to know each of
the people I mentioned in these

01:01:10.401 --> 01:01:11.670
three collaborations,
really well.

01:01:11.671 --> 01:01:13.160
And sometimes the fun
doesn't involve work.

01:01:13.161 --> 01:01:17.020
Sometimes they're connoisseurs
of fine wine and food as well.

01:01:17.021 --> 01:01:20.788
But a few thoughts, particularly
for younger people in the field.

01:01:20.789 --> 01:01:22.598
One lesson you might get
from this talk is that,

01:01:22.599 --> 01:01:24.770
I've done a little bit of
work in network optimization,

01:01:24.771 --> 01:01:26.580
a little bit of work in
programming languages,

01:01:26.581 --> 01:01:28.450
a little bit of work in
compact data structures.

01:01:28.451 --> 01:01:30.470
And in fact, I've really done
very little work in any of

01:01:30.471 --> 01:01:32.800
those, because I've worked
a lot with collaborators who

01:01:32.801 --> 01:01:35.100
had expertise in those and
other areas.

01:01:35.101 --> 01:01:36.220
So I've been pretty
opportunistic.

01:01:36.221 --> 01:01:38.090
If someone will play with me,
and they have a hammer that's

01:01:38.091 --> 01:01:41.550
relevant to the problems
I work on, I say yes.

01:01:41.551 --> 01:01:43.010
That's not always
the right way to go.

01:01:43.011 --> 01:01:44.803
In particular,
there can be merit and

01:01:44.804 --> 01:01:47.607
honing ones own single hammer
to use on a range of problems,

01:01:47.608 --> 01:01:50.708
particularly for grad students
or people early in their career.

01:01:50.709 --> 01:01:53.486
It gives you a way to control
your fate, and to do work

01:01:53.487 --> 01:01:56.533
without being dependent on
a collaborator at all times.

01:01:56.534 --> 01:01:58.823
It requires a bit of care
because you end up having to

01:01:58.824 --> 01:02:01.328
pick that hammer very wisely
because becoming an expert

01:02:01.329 --> 01:02:02.245
takes a lot of time.

01:02:02.246 --> 01:02:04.349
And you wanna make sure
that hammer is useful for

01:02:04.350 --> 01:02:06.722
a wide range of problems in
your field, not just one.

01:02:06.723 --> 01:02:09.830
You can do what I did, which
is to work by collaboration.

01:02:09.831 --> 01:02:12.970
It's riskier for junior folks,
but it can be okay

01:02:12.971 --> 01:02:15.800
if you can define a niche for
yourself involving digging

01:02:15.801 --> 01:02:19.960
really deep into crystallizing
models of what the computational

01:02:19.961 --> 01:02:21.910
capabilities are of
the devices in your own field.

01:02:21.911 --> 01:02:24.417
And that's sort of where I've
tried to walk the line between

01:02:24.418 --> 01:02:26.541
deeply understanding
the capabilities below me.

01:02:26.542 --> 01:02:28.669
And the network administrative
tasks above me and

01:02:28.670 --> 01:02:30.744
translate those into terms
that other people and

01:02:30.745 --> 01:02:32.320
other fields can think about.

01:02:32.321 --> 01:02:34.920
But it's a bit more of
a nebulous skill set than being

01:02:34.921 --> 01:02:36.970
an expert in a particular
disciplinary domain, so

01:02:36.971 --> 01:02:39.530
certainly it's something
to do with caution.

01:02:39.531 --> 01:02:41.350
There can also be a pretty
steep learning curve,

01:02:41.351 --> 01:02:43.520
many of these projects I just
mentioned were multi-year

01:02:43.521 --> 01:02:44.940
efforts before
anything happened.

01:02:44.941 --> 01:02:48.290
That was interesting, because
often there's a lot of time

01:02:48.291 --> 01:02:51.070
taken to learn the language
of both of the two different

01:02:51.071 --> 01:02:53.130
fields, and
the two different cultures.

01:02:53.131 --> 01:02:53.810
And in some cases,

01:02:53.811 --> 01:02:56.245
it can be easier in one
direction than the other.

01:02:56.246 --> 01:02:58.209
Just in working with
programming languages,

01:02:58.210 --> 01:03:00.626
colleagues have certainly
found it's been easier to get

01:03:00.627 --> 01:03:03.295
programming languages students
to do great work in networking

01:03:03.296 --> 01:03:04.474
than the other way around.

01:03:04.475 --> 01:03:06.770
Because PL has many
years of training and

01:03:06.771 --> 01:03:09.740
a lot of aesthetic involved
in it that can be very hard

01:03:09.741 --> 01:03:12.440
to train in a short
period of time.

01:03:12.441 --> 01:03:15.450
Different subsets of networking
can themselves be challenging,

01:03:15.451 --> 01:03:17.278
but they're often
things you learn more

01:03:17.279 --> 01:03:18.470
incrementally as you go.

01:03:18.471 --> 01:03:20.725
And you can often do interesting
work without knowing everything

01:03:20.726 --> 01:03:21.428
about networking.

01:03:21.429 --> 01:03:24.074
So I certainly found that it's
sometimes easier to go from

01:03:24.075 --> 01:03:26.920
the hammer towards the nail
than the other way around.

01:03:26.921 --> 01:03:29.780
And one way to walk that line
is to join an existing community

01:03:29.781 --> 01:03:32.220
of people in a networking PL

01:03:32.221 --> 01:03:33.500
intersection is
an example of that.

01:03:33.501 --> 01:03:35.690
Now, where there's already
some technical foundation,

01:03:35.691 --> 01:03:38.750
software stacks, models to think
of, of course, it's always with

01:03:38.751 --> 01:03:40.710
some risk, because if you
join the party too late,

01:03:40.711 --> 01:03:42.740
it's not as necessarily
as interesting.

01:03:42.741 --> 01:03:45.458
But sometimes there can be the
beginnings of a new community

01:03:45.459 --> 01:03:48.120
that afford an opportunity to
do new work without doing all

01:03:48.121 --> 01:03:49.497
the heavy lifting yourself.

01:03:49.498 --> 01:03:51.733
One thing I hear a lot
from people is credit.

01:03:51.734 --> 01:03:54.370
How does this work when you're
publishing in two different

01:03:54.371 --> 01:03:56.790
venues and
there's may authors on papers?

01:03:56.791 --> 01:03:58.178
And I would just say
a couple lessons there.

01:03:58.179 --> 01:04:00.923
I've worked in these projects
with a number of junior

01:04:00.924 --> 01:04:03.913
collaborators, and clearly one
way to walk that line is to

01:04:03.914 --> 01:04:07.270
publish where it is appropriate
for the junior collaborator, so

01:04:07.271 --> 01:04:10.259
that they're not taking on a
risk that you no longer have to

01:04:10.260 --> 01:04:11.390
worry about taking.

01:04:11.391 --> 01:04:14.290
And also understanding the
evaluation processes of one's

01:04:14.291 --> 01:04:16.805
own institution so
you know whether the work

01:04:16.806 --> 01:04:19.365
that's done with multiple
senior or multiple faculty

01:04:19.366 --> 01:04:22.988
co-authors will be valued in
the tenure evaluation process.

01:04:22.989 --> 01:04:25.240
And finally, I'll just note that
sometimes interdisciplinary work

01:04:25.241 --> 01:04:26.870
can make this problem easier.

01:04:26.871 --> 01:04:28.316
If I write the paper with Nate,

01:04:28.317 --> 01:04:31.211
even when he was a relatively
junior colleague, it was really

01:04:31.212 --> 01:04:33.956
really obvious which work was
his and which work was mine.

01:04:33.957 --> 01:04:36.525
Cuz there's just stuff in the
paper that I could never have

01:04:36.526 --> 01:04:38.268
done myself that I
barely understand.

01:04:38.269 --> 01:04:40.086
Now, if he is working
with a PL colleague,

01:04:40.087 --> 01:04:41.246
it is a little less clear and

01:04:41.247 --> 01:04:44.023
likewise if I'm working with a
junior networking person, it can

01:04:44.024 --> 01:04:46.410
be a little less clear who is
bringing what to the table.

01:04:46.411 --> 01:04:49.000
But within a disciplinary work
it's often pretty easy to tell

01:04:49.001 --> 01:04:50.740
whose fingerprints are on
which parts of the work.

01:04:50.741 --> 01:04:53.099
&gt;&gt; James Bond was [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

01:04:53.100 --> 01:04:53.634
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

01:04:53.635 --> 01:04:55.544
&gt;&gt; Exactly, bingo, bingo.

01:04:55.545 --> 01:04:58.410
Yeah, I remember when my
colleague Jen Fagamom once said

01:04:58.411 --> 01:05:00.738
to me she said,
networking papers are weird,

01:05:00.739 --> 01:05:02.183
they have a lot text, yeah.

01:05:02.184 --> 01:05:09.242
[LAUGH] So there's truth
in that, I did the text.

01:05:09.243 --> 01:05:10.795
So I think another
piece is helping

01:05:10.796 --> 01:05:13.430
interdisciplinary collaborations
can be challenging.

01:05:13.431 --> 01:05:16.271
I only talked about three
projects that were at least to

01:05:16.272 --> 01:05:17.644
some degree successful.

01:05:17.645 --> 01:05:18.890
There were a tons
of projects I chose

01:05:18.891 --> 01:05:20.960
not to talk about
because they weren't.

01:05:20.961 --> 01:05:22.790
And reasons they
weren't successful

01:05:22.791 --> 01:05:25.198
was often collaborators
don't stick together.

01:05:25.199 --> 01:05:27.830
It's a relationship that
requires nurturing, and

01:05:27.831 --> 01:05:29.861
also people that
are willing to dig in for

01:05:29.862 --> 01:05:32.998
a long period of time before
something interesting happens.

01:05:32.999 --> 01:05:35.069
And the bottomline is,
sometimes the work isn't

01:05:35.070 --> 01:05:37.338
actually interesting in both
fields where there can be

01:05:37.339 --> 01:05:38.927
an expertise I need
to get my job done.

01:05:38.928 --> 01:05:41.478
But if that work is not
interesting intellectually to

01:05:41.479 --> 01:05:44.084
the other person because
it's sort of routine, run of

01:05:44.085 --> 01:05:46.860
the mill, that's not gonna
be a lasting collaboration,

01:05:46.861 --> 01:05:48.957
unless they really have
a lot of patience for

01:05:48.958 --> 01:05:50.109
tech transfer, yeah?

01:05:50.110 --> 01:05:52.705
&gt;&gt; I think even when it
is an exciting field,

01:05:52.706 --> 01:05:56.427
sometimes it's hard to convince
the people in your field.

01:05:56.428 --> 01:05:59.511
On the intersection, if you're
trying to publish in POPL,

01:05:59.512 --> 01:06:02.484
the people who review the papers
look at the POPL results.

01:06:02.485 --> 01:06:05.696
And they don't know networking
so they don't understand fully

01:06:05.697 --> 01:06:07.781
what the challenges are,
how hard it is.

01:06:07.782 --> 01:06:11.308
So I think there's a certain
amount of just assuming that,

01:06:11.309 --> 01:06:15.046
if it's this collaboration it's
a weaker form of the same kind

01:06:15.047 --> 01:06:15.693
of thing.

01:06:15.694 --> 01:06:17.603
&gt;&gt; Yeah, particularly in
the work with Mung that ended up

01:06:17.604 --> 01:06:19.472
being an issue on the
optimization work I talked about

01:06:19.473 --> 01:06:20.150
in the beginning.

01:06:20.151 --> 01:06:22.093
Cuz networking conferences
would read it and

01:06:22.094 --> 01:06:24.370
be like,
there's scary math in it.

01:06:24.371 --> 01:06:26.678
And then the optimization
venues would be like,

01:06:26.679 --> 01:06:28.509
this is standard
optimization theory.

01:06:28.510 --> 01:06:31.700
Because we're trying to
pick problem formulations

01:06:31.701 --> 01:06:33.880
that were amenable
to simple math.

01:06:33.881 --> 01:06:36.286
Simple math, for
the optimization folks.

01:06:36.287 --> 01:06:38.547
Right, cuz we wanted
everything to be convex so

01:06:38.548 --> 01:06:40.590
we could do optimization
decomposition.

01:06:40.591 --> 01:06:42.250
But in my own community
there's Greek in it,

01:06:42.251 --> 01:06:43.840
so they're like I don't know.

01:06:43.841 --> 01:06:45.760
What you guys are up to,
it seems a little weird.

01:06:45.761 --> 01:06:49.077
So that definitely had some
issues because of differences in

01:06:49.078 --> 01:06:50.122
value structure.

01:06:50.123 --> 01:06:53.554
And in fact, another piece of
that is some communities value

01:06:53.555 --> 01:06:56.461
proofs and some value code and
not many value both.

01:06:56.462 --> 01:06:58.471
And so if you want
the paper to have both,

01:06:58.472 --> 01:07:01.487
if you want to sort of make the
more applied people and the more

01:07:01.488 --> 01:07:04.700
theoretical people happy, that
can certainly be a challenge.

01:07:04.701 --> 01:07:05.201
And at a minimum,

01:07:05.201 --> 01:07:08.230
the collaborators need a shared
union of each other's value,

01:07:08.231 --> 01:07:08.920
if you will, or

01:07:08.921 --> 01:07:12.130
union of their values,
to be able to make that work.

01:07:12.131 --> 01:07:14.470
And of course,
engaging students and post docs.

01:07:14.471 --> 01:07:16.340
I found this is actually
generally pretty good in

01:07:16.341 --> 01:07:18.120
the sense that students,
often are excited about

01:07:18.121 --> 01:07:20.850
interdisciplinary work, because
it is exciting, it is a little

01:07:20.851 --> 01:07:23.940
risky, but it is unique, it
gives them a niche to be in but,

01:07:23.941 --> 01:07:26.550
it does require more
training and more patience.

01:07:26.551 --> 01:07:28.613
And wrangling two or
more advisor's,

01:07:28.614 --> 01:07:32.140
which is always a challenge of
a non-technical nature as well.

01:07:32.141 --> 01:07:35.080
Finally, I know this is going
to sound kind of weird for

01:07:35.081 --> 01:07:36.440
an Internet researcher to say,
but

01:07:36.441 --> 01:07:38.800
I'm a big fan of
physical proximity.

01:07:38.801 --> 01:07:42.390
This kind of work, all
happened in person, all of it.

01:07:42.391 --> 01:07:44.860
The unsuccessful projects were
a mix of in person and not, but

01:07:44.861 --> 01:07:47.810
it's just super hard when
two faculty members or

01:07:47.811 --> 01:07:51.206
two researchers, really need to
have air time with one another.

01:07:51.207 --> 01:07:53.340
It's just very, very difficult
to have those early stages of

01:07:53.341 --> 01:07:55.030
collaboration take
place not in person.

01:07:56.390 --> 01:07:58.190
This has worked for me remotely,

01:07:58.191 --> 01:07:59.500
when a student does
an internship and

01:07:59.501 --> 01:08:01.720
I had many successful projects
at Microsoft of this type,

01:08:01.721 --> 01:08:04.250
where students spent a summer or
longer at Microsoft,

01:08:04.251 --> 01:08:07.130
and made bridges stronger
to make that possible.

01:08:07.131 --> 01:08:09.440
But for projects where it's like
two faculty members at different

01:08:09.441 --> 01:08:12.340
institutions working,
it's just kind of a blood bath,

01:08:12.341 --> 01:08:15.250
because people just,
out of sight, out of mind and

01:08:15.251 --> 01:08:18.920
it's just hard to talk on Skype
or hangouts all the time.

01:08:18.921 --> 01:08:20.980
So I'll just conclude by saying,
computer networking,

01:08:20.981 --> 01:08:23.210
I've always found it a very
exciting area of research,

01:08:23.211 --> 01:08:25.970
because it's a rich space of
important practical problems

01:08:25.971 --> 01:08:27.710
that affect real
people's live's, and

01:08:27.711 --> 01:08:30.670
as an intellectually rich
space of really hard problems.

01:08:30.671 --> 01:08:33.180
And the sort of general grand
challenge of building networks

01:08:33.181 --> 01:08:36.080
worthy of the trust society
increasingly puts on them,

01:08:36.081 --> 01:08:38.980
is intellectually enriching and
has really I think potential for

01:08:38.981 --> 01:08:39.960
a lot of practical value.

01:08:39.961 --> 01:08:43.570
But it definitely I think
requires ability to bridge

01:08:43.571 --> 01:08:45.710
across the divides with some of
these neighboring disciplines

01:08:45.711 --> 01:08:48.590
within computer science and
elsewhere to make it happen.

01:08:48.591 --> 01:08:49.430
So thanks for your attention,

01:08:50.670 --> 01:08:53.763
I'm also happy to
take questions.

01:08:53.764 --> 01:08:56.557
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah?

01:08:56.558 --> 01:09:01.480
&gt;&gt; I like the metaphor hammer,
you only know what hammer

01:09:01.481 --> 01:09:04.450
you need after you decide
what to do, right?

01:09:04.451 --> 01:09:07.500
I'm more curious about,
in this year how

01:09:07.501 --> 01:09:10.550
you build up your vision,
and how do you decide

01:09:10.551 --> 01:09:13.310
which directive is better than
the other so you would jump?

01:09:13.311 --> 01:09:15.880
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think in particular
for the first two projects,

01:09:15.881 --> 01:09:18.370
I've always been interested in
resource optimization questions.

01:09:18.371 --> 01:09:19.400
A lot of the work
that I did at AT&amp;T,

01:09:19.401 --> 01:09:21.910
was always about optimizing
resources and about

01:09:21.911 --> 01:09:24.480
the pain network administrators
felt in getting their networks

01:09:24.481 --> 01:09:25.600
to do what they wanted.

01:09:25.601 --> 01:09:28.796
So optimization and PL, they
just sort of jump out as hey,

01:09:28.797 --> 01:09:31.473
there's pain around
resource optimization and

01:09:31.474 --> 01:09:33.330
around expression.

01:09:33.331 --> 01:09:35.590
And it became sort of natural
to think of those two fields.

01:09:35.591 --> 01:09:36.430
And then, I think recently,

01:09:36.431 --> 01:09:38.200
with these new emerging
hardware devices,

01:09:38.201 --> 01:09:40.950
it's really clear that
it's running a streaming

01:09:40.951 --> 01:09:43.780
algorithm with limited
storage and computation.

01:09:43.781 --> 01:09:47.570
And so then you're like,
that's compact data structures.

01:09:47.571 --> 01:09:48.830
So I think for

01:09:48.831 --> 01:09:51.320
those three particular projects
it was sort of natural.

01:09:51.321 --> 01:09:53.590
I think it could be
harder in other areas.

01:09:53.591 --> 01:09:55.070
But I think it's not
a coincidence, that those

01:09:55.071 --> 01:09:58.150
particular problems were born
out of networking, people

01:09:58.151 --> 01:10:01.570
are always preoccupied with
problems of scarcity, right?

01:10:01.571 --> 01:10:04.150
And problems of scarcity really
benefit from algorithms and

01:10:04.151 --> 01:10:04.770
optimization.

01:10:04.771 --> 01:10:06.460
So that's just like
our bread and butter.

01:10:06.461 --> 01:10:07.985
And the PL stuff is
a little more of a stretch,

01:10:07.986 --> 01:10:09.675
because I've never
take a PL course.

01:10:09.676 --> 01:10:10.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I've actually never

01:10:10.761 --> 01:10:12.081
taken a networking
course either.

01:10:12.082 --> 01:10:14.335
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Either, for that matter,

01:10:14.336 --> 01:10:15.170
actually.

01:10:15.171 --> 01:10:16.840
But, in fact,

01:10:16.841 --> 01:10:19.180
actually some of my colleagues
who are more disparaging of

01:10:19.181 --> 01:10:21.040
networking think that might
be why I stayed in the field.

01:10:21.041 --> 01:10:22.720
That if I'd actually taken
a networking course,

01:10:22.721 --> 01:10:24.250
I might think differently
about the field.

01:10:24.251 --> 01:10:26.890
But yeah, so
I think some of it is.

01:10:26.891 --> 01:10:29.500
Those are sort of the most
natural collaborations.

01:10:29.501 --> 01:10:31.850
I think the Eric's point's about
game theory and economics.

01:10:31.851 --> 01:10:33.960
I think that's an area
that's natural, too, but

01:10:33.961 --> 01:10:36.119
that's tougher,
cuz I think it's a bigger reach,

01:10:36.120 --> 01:10:38.720
I think, for networking people
who don't already have some of

01:10:38.721 --> 01:10:39.525
that background.

01:10:39.526 --> 01:10:41.242
Whereas some of these
stay within CS,

01:10:41.243 --> 01:10:43.871
and I do think there's starting
to be a more vibrant sort of

01:10:43.872 --> 01:10:47.003
mechanism design and algorithmic
game theory community within CS.

01:10:47.004 --> 01:10:48.053
But before that built up,

01:10:48.054 --> 01:10:49.532
I think it was
actually really hard,

01:10:49.533 --> 01:10:52.730
cuz there's an even bigger
gap to talk to an economist.

01:10:52.731 --> 01:10:53.430
Like, what is that?

01:10:53.431 --> 01:10:55.780
I don't even know how
to do that, yeah?

01:10:56.890 --> 01:10:59.728
&gt;&gt; The one thing that I think
is interesting about this

01:10:59.729 --> 01:11:02.896
relationship with EPL networking
is there's sort of a,

01:11:02.897 --> 01:11:05.272
the history of PL and
hardware design, and

01:11:05.273 --> 01:11:06.861
that evolved over decades.

01:11:06.862 --> 01:11:09.623
And then the network community
sort of saw that there was

01:11:09.624 --> 01:11:12.740
a potential opportunity there,
a similar relationship.

01:11:12.741 --> 01:11:15.360
But they had all that history
to sort of quickly move up,

01:11:15.361 --> 01:11:17.340
and get on par with.

01:11:17.341 --> 01:11:20.040
And so I think it's
exciting to see the kind

01:11:20.041 --> 01:11:22.010
of rapid development
of these ideas.

01:11:22.011 --> 01:11:24.006
And how they get
applied in practice.

01:11:24.007 --> 01:11:25.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think part of that,

01:11:25.331 --> 01:11:28.259
going back to points that Victor
was making, was because there

01:11:28.260 --> 01:11:31.298
was an inflection point in the
networking community with Cloud,

01:11:31.299 --> 01:11:35.230
with merchant sets that changed
the nature of the question.

01:11:35.231 --> 01:11:38.030
So it was possible to build on
top of something that wasn't

01:11:38.031 --> 01:11:38.600
there before.

01:11:39.650 --> 01:11:41.194
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I just want to add to that,

01:11:41.195 --> 01:11:43.820
I think that it's
actually really correct.

01:11:43.821 --> 01:11:46.830
For example, if you thinking
by the cloud, I just,

01:11:46.831 --> 01:11:49.520
I think you saw my blog,
I wrote recently.

01:11:49.521 --> 01:11:50.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; I think,

01:11:50.361 --> 01:11:53.180
things are getting so
complicated.

01:11:53.181 --> 01:11:55.432
I mean so complex.

01:11:55.433 --> 01:11:59.150
In here, we live actually in
a very pristine world in some

01:11:59.151 --> 01:12:01.410
sense where every kids
are thinking clear of things.

01:12:01.411 --> 01:12:04.678
I just had lunch with
a friend on the other side,

01:12:04.679 --> 01:12:06.813
and he sort of explained to me.

01:12:06.814 --> 01:12:07.666
&gt;&gt; The other side of what?

01:12:07.667 --> 01:12:08.578
&gt;&gt; Side of what?

01:12:08.579 --> 01:12:14.940
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

01:12:14.941 --> 01:12:16.927
&gt;&gt; Okay [CROSSTALK]

01:12:16.928 --> 01:12:20.456
&gt;&gt; I was thinking of someone in

01:12:20.457 --> 01:12:24.796
prison or [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Anyway so the point was that,

01:12:24.797 --> 01:12:27.430
dealing with that complexity.

01:12:27.431 --> 01:12:31.125
I mean, it's sort of like, it's
always a firefighting drill,

01:12:31.126 --> 01:12:32.230
it's non-stop.

01:12:32.231 --> 01:12:33.810
Now how much capacity
do you have?

01:12:33.811 --> 01:12:37.320
How do you sort of
move VMs around?

01:12:38.950 --> 01:12:41.210
And networks actually, and
things go down all the time.

01:12:41.211 --> 01:12:43.363
The problem is,
that if one of, as you know,

01:12:43.364 --> 01:12:45.519
one router goes down,
one switch goes down,

01:12:45.520 --> 01:12:48.642
then entire massive sections of
the cloud come down, which means

01:12:48.643 --> 01:12:50.955
all these properties,
everything comes out.

01:12:50.956 --> 01:12:54.241
So, as we progress, it's
becoming even more clearer that

01:12:54.242 --> 01:12:57.002
we should have done this
right in the first place,

01:12:57.003 --> 01:13:00.092
which is that we should not
have acted altogether the way

01:13:00.093 --> 01:13:01.650
we've acted altogether.

01:13:01.651 --> 01:13:06.450
We should have had some sort of
discipline in the way we did,

01:13:06.451 --> 01:13:08.100
we took conceptual programming,
and so

01:13:08.101 --> 01:13:10.830
this is what's going on with net
verification now for example,

01:13:10.831 --> 01:13:12.340
right all this work,
that's going on.

01:13:12.341 --> 01:13:14.438
&gt;&gt; Yep, and I think the other
important point about cloud

01:13:14.439 --> 01:13:16.901
companies, Microsoft included
the network is just one piece of

01:13:16.902 --> 01:13:19.501
the infrastructure, and in every
other part of the infrastructure

01:13:19.502 --> 01:13:21.780
you're allowed to write your
own code and touch the code and

01:13:21.781 --> 01:13:23.398
that somehow the network
is sacrosanct.

01:13:23.399 --> 01:13:25.887
The network's in the way and
you can't fix it.

01:13:25.888 --> 01:13:27.380
And so people just defend it.

01:13:27.381 --> 01:13:29.570
Whereas, I think, if you work
at a networking company,

01:13:29.571 --> 01:13:31.852
that's just so normal to you,
but if you were in a company,

01:13:31.853 --> 01:13:33.587
where networking is just
one of many pieces,

01:13:33.588 --> 01:13:34.843
that's just such an anathema.

01:13:34.844 --> 01:13:39.261
&gt;&gt; Besides, what I wanted to say
was that will keep us busy for

01:13:39.262 --> 01:13:40.250
a while.

01:13:40.251 --> 01:13:42.047
There's no question about it.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:13:42.048 --> 01:13:43.997
But still there are other
aspects to things too.

01:13:43.998 --> 01:13:46.115
Like for example you
know latency is a thing,

01:13:46.116 --> 01:13:47.578
think latency the first time.

01:13:47.579 --> 01:13:48.684
You're getting faster and
faster.

01:13:48.685 --> 01:13:49.426
We don't really

01:13:49.427 --> 01:13:51.814
know why the speed we can't
get to the speed of light.

01:13:51.815 --> 01:13:53.537
Like why can't we move pack and

01:13:53.538 --> 01:13:56.261
star fast enough to actually
be able to get to it.

01:13:56.262 --> 01:13:59.438
So there are all these other
things that require it, but

01:13:59.439 --> 01:14:02.760
the balance is where our
attention is right now.

01:14:02.761 --> 01:14:06.920
The whole community seemed
to have that intention of.

01:14:06.921 --> 01:14:11.640
When you get to things like I
don't quite have a clarity on

01:14:11.641 --> 01:14:14.730
what sort of interdisciplinary
sort of stuff can help there and

01:14:14.731 --> 01:14:16.060
things of that nature.

01:14:16.061 --> 01:14:18.230
&gt;&gt; One thing I've not worked
on that seems very natural

01:14:18.231 --> 01:14:19.110
also is control theory.

01:14:19.111 --> 01:14:21.394
I mean, I talked a lot about
control loops in this talk, but

01:14:21.395 --> 01:14:24.048
I've never actually collaborated
with someone in control theory,

01:14:24.049 --> 01:14:25.803
and that seems like
a missing link, as well.

01:14:25.804 --> 01:14:27.876
Or I think the networking
community there are a handful

01:14:27.877 --> 01:14:29.408
of people who do
like Muhammad or

01:14:29.409 --> 01:14:30.816
MIT certainly has
done a lot of work.

01:14:30.817 --> 01:14:31.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:14:31.331 --> 01:14:33.889
&gt;&gt; Including work he did here,
that has that tasty.

01:14:33.890 --> 01:14:35.471
So there's certainly
people who do it, but

01:14:35.472 --> 01:14:37.765
I think that's an under explored
area in networking for sure.

01:14:37.766 --> 01:14:39.856
Because everything we
do is control loops and

01:14:39.857 --> 01:14:42.717
complex interacting ones, and
when they interact in ways you

01:14:42.718 --> 01:14:44.710
don't understand all
hell breaks loose.

01:14:44.711 --> 01:14:48.340
Yeah.

01:14:48.341 --> 01:14:51.790
Any other comments, questions? Good.

01:14:51.791 --> 01:14:52.360
Thanks so much.

01:14:52.361 --> 01:14:52.941
&gt;&gt; Sure.
Thanks.

01:14:52.942 --> 01:14:59.755
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

